// What each country card says. Written for Been There; one place per line so it is easy to edit.
export default {
  "AD": {"name":"Andorra","capital":"Andorra la Vella","continent":"Europe","missing":"A Pyrenean valley country with a Romanesque church in nearly every village and trails that start at the bus stop.","dontMiss":["The Romanesque church of Sant Joan de Caselles","The climb to Comapedrosa, Andorra’s highest peak","The Madriu-Perafita-Claror valley, a UNESCO site"],"lookFor":"Marmots whistling from the boulder fields of high valleys like Sorteny, in summer.","eat":"Trinxat: mashed potato and cabbage fried with bacon, mountain food for cold days.","fact":"Andorra’s two heads of state are the Bishop of Urgell, in Spain, and whoever is President of France."},
  "AE": {"name":"United Arab Emirates","capital":"Abu Dhabi","continent":"Asia","missing":"Dunes at the edge of the Empty Quarter, old trading creeks behind the towers, and the Hajar mountains in the east.","dontMiss":["The Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque in Abu Dhabi","An abra across Dubai Creek to the spice souq","The dunes of the Liwa Oasis"],"lookFor":"Flamingos at Ras Al Khor in Dubai, within sight of the skyline, mostly in winter.","eat":"Machboos, spiced rice with meat, and luqaimat, fried dumplings drizzled with date syrup.","fact":"The Burj Khalifa is tall enough to watch the sun set from the ground, then ride up and watch it set again."},
  "AF": {"name":"Afghanistan","capital":"Kabul","continent":"Asia","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: the deep blue lakes of Band-e Amir, the Hindu Kush, and a guest culture taken very seriously.","dontMiss":["Band-e Amir’s chain of deep blue lakes","The Buddha niches in the cliffs at Bamiyan","The Minaret of Jam, alone in a remote river gorge"],"lookFor":"Marco Polo sheep, with huge spiralled horns, on the high Pamir pastures of the Wakhan Corridor in summer.","eat":"Kabuli pulao: rice cooked with lamb and topped with sweet carrots and raisins, the national dish.","fact":"Murals in Bamiyan’s caves, from about the 7th century, are among the oldest known oil paintings."},
  "AG": {"name":"Antigua and Barbuda","capital":"St. John’s","continent":"North America","missing":"A different beach for every day, Nelson’s 18th-century dockyard still working as a marina, and Barbuda’s near-empty pink sand.","dontMiss":["Nelson’s Dockyard in English Harbour","Sunday evening at the Shirley Heights lookout","Barbuda’s Codrington Lagoon by boat"],"lookFor":"Magnificent frigatebirds in Barbuda’s Codrington Lagoon; males puff up red throat pouches in the breeding season.","eat":"Fungie and pepperpot: a cornmeal and okra pudding with a thick stew of greens and meat.","fact":"Nelson’s Dockyard is the only continuously working Georgian dockyard in the world."},
  "AL": {"name":"Albania","capital":"Tirana","continent":"Europe","missing":"An Ionian coast without the crowds, Ottoman stone towns, and the mountain trail from Valbona to Theth.","dontMiss":["The day hike from Valbona over the pass to Theth","Gjirokastër’s stone-roofed old town and castle","Butrint’s ruins beside a lagoon near Sarandë"],"lookFor":"Dalmatian pelicans nesting on Karavasta Lagoon, in Divjakë-Karavasta National Park, in spring.","eat":"Tavë kosi: lamb and rice baked under a tangy crust of yogurt and egg.","fact":"Under Enver Hoxha, Albania built about 173,000 concrete bunkers for an invasion that never came."},
  "AM": {"name":"Armenia","capital":"Yerevan","continent":"Asia","missing":"Monasteries on cliff edges, a capital built of pink volcanic stone, and some of the oldest winemaking on record.","dontMiss":["Geghard Monastery, partly carved into the mountain","Tatev Monastery via the Wings of Tatev cable car","Lake Sevan and the Sevanavank churches"],"lookFor":"Apricot trees in the Ararat Valley’s orchards; the fruit ripens in late June and July.","eat":"Khorovats, Armenian barbecue, with lavash baked on the walls of a clay tonir oven.","fact":"Armenia made Christianity its state religion in 301 AD, usually counted as the first country to do so."},
  "AO": {"name":"Angola","capital":"Luanda","continent":"Africa","missing":"Huge waterfalls, a desert that runs into the Atlantic, and Luanda’s seafront, in a country few tourists have reached.","dontMiss":["Kalandula Falls on the Lucala River","The eroded red cliffs of Miradouro da Lua","The Namib desert coast around Namibe"],"lookFor":"Welwitschia, a desert plant that can live more than a thousand years, in the Namib near Namibe.","eat":"Moamba de galinha: chicken stewed in palm oil with okra and garlic, served with funge.","fact":"Angola’s giant sable antelope was feared extinct after the civil war until camera traps found a herd in 2005."},
  "AQ": {"name":"Antarctica","capital":null,"continent":"Antarctica","missing":"Ice to the horizon, penguins that pay you no attention, and a silence you won’t find anywhere else.","dontMiss":["A landing on the Antarctic Peninsula","The Lemaire Channel between its ice cliffs","Deception Island’s flooded volcanic caldera"],"lookFor":"Gentoo, chinstrap and Adélie penguins along the Peninsula, November to March.","eat":"There’s no local food; on the ships it’s hot chocolate after a cold Zodiac landing.","fact":"Antarctica’s ice sheet holds about 70% of the world’s fresh water."},
  "AR": {"name":"Argentina","capital":"Buenos Aires","continent":"South America","missing":"Patagonian glaciers you can hear calving, wine country under the Andes, and Buenos Aires’s late dinners and tango halls.","dontMiss":["Perito Moreno Glacier, near El Calafate","Iguazú Falls from the Argentine side’s catwalks","Hiking toward Mount Fitz Roy from El Chaltén"],"lookFor":"Southern right whales off Península Valdés, often close to shore, from about June to December.","eat":"Asado: beef and offal grilled slowly over wood embers, the Sunday ritual.","fact":"Aconcagua, at 6,961 m, is the highest mountain outside Asia."},
  "AT": {"name":"Austria","capital":"Vienna","continent":"Europe","missing":"Coffeehouses where one coffee buys you an afternoon, mountain huts that serve hot lunches, and Mozart’s Salzburg.","dontMiss":["An afternoon in a Viennese coffeehouse with the papers","Hallstatt and its lake, out of season","The Grossglockner High Alpine Road in summer"],"lookFor":"Alpine marmots by the paths at Kaiser-Franz-Josefs-Höhe, below the Grossglockner, in summer.","eat":"Wiener schnitzel: veal pounded thin, breaded and fried, with potato salad and lemon.","fact":"Vienna’s Tiergarten Schönbrunn, founded in 1752, is the oldest zoo still operating."},
  "AU": {"name":"Australia","capital":"Canberra","continent":"Oceania","missing":"The outback’s red distances, reefs an hour’s boat ride from town, and animals that exist nowhere else.","dontMiss":["Snorkeling the Great Barrier Reef from Cairns or Port Douglas","Uluru at sunrise, and the walk around its base","The coastal walk from Bondi to Coogee in Sydney"],"lookFor":"Quokkas around the settlement and bays of Rottnest Island, off Perth, all year.","eat":"A meat pie with tomato sauce from a country bakery, or barramundi off the barbecue.","fact":"Australia is wider than the Moon: about 4,000 km east to west, against the Moon’s 3,474 km."},
  "AW": {"name":"Aruba","capital":"Oranjestad","continent":"North America","missing":"Dry sunny weather all year, white beaches on the calm side, and a rough desert coast on the other.","dontMiss":["Eagle Beach","The Natural Pool in Arikok National Park","Snorkelling the Antilla shipwreck"],"lookFor":"Divi-divi trees, bent by the trade winds so they all point southwest.","eat":"Keshi yena, a round of cheese stuffed with spiced chicken or beef and baked.","fact":"Aruba sits south of the main hurricane belt, so storms rarely hit it."},
  "AZ": {"name":"Azerbaijan","capital":"Baku","continent":"Asia","missing":"Mud volcanoes, a hillside that has burned for decades, and Baku’s walled old town under glass towers.","dontMiss":["Baku’s Old City and the Palace of the Shirvanshahs","The petroglyphs and mud volcanoes of Gobustan","The Khan’s Palace and caravanserai in Sheki"],"lookFor":"Goitered gazelles on the semi-desert plains of Shirvan National Park, south of Baku.","eat":"Plov: saffron rice with lamb, chestnuts and dried fruit, sometimes baked in a lavash crust.","fact":"Azerbaijan has more mud volcanoes than any other country, by most counts about 350."},
  "BA": {"name":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","capital":"Sarajevo","continent":"Europe","missing":"Ottoman bazaars and Austro-Hungarian cafés on the same street, river canyons, and coffee served with ceremony.","dontMiss":["Sarajevo’s Baščaršija bazaar, with a Bosnian coffee","Mostar’s Old Bridge and the divers who jump from it","The Blagaj Tekke at the source of the Buna"],"lookFor":"Perućica, one of Europe’s last primeval forests, with beeches and firs over 50 m tall, in Sutjeska National Park.","eat":"Ćevapi: small grilled minced-meat sausages in somun flatbread with raw onion and kajmak.","fact":"Sarajevo had electric trams in 1895, two years before Vienna, its imperial capital."},
  "BB": {"name":"Barbados","capital":"Bridgetown","continent":"North America","missing":"Rum shops on every corner, a calm west coast, and a wild Atlantic east coast with almost nobody on it.","dontMiss":["The Friday night fish fry at Oistins","The east coast at Bathsheba and the Soup Bowl","Harrison’s Cave by underground tram"],"lookFor":"Green monkeys, brought from West Africa in the 1600s, in the gullies and at the Barbados Wildlife Reserve.","eat":"Cou-cou and flying fish: cornmeal and okra with steamed fish in a spicy sauce, the national dish.","fact":"George Washington’s only trip outside mainland North America was to Barbados, in 1751."},
  "BD": {"name":"Bangladesh","capital":"Dhaka","continent":"Asia","missing":"River life by ferry and rickshaw, and the Sundarbans mangroves, where tigers still swim between islands.","dontMiss":["A boat trip into the Sundarbans mangroves","Old Dhaka and the Sadarghat river port at dawn","The tea gardens around Sreemangal"],"lookFor":"Spotted deer and rhesus macaques along the Sundarbans creeks; tigers mostly leave just pugmarks.","eat":"Ilish, the national fish, cooked in mustard paste, best in the monsoon.","fact":"UNESCO’s International Mother Language Day, 21 February, honors students killed in Dhaka in 1952 defending Bengali."},
  "BE": {"name":"Belgium","capital":"Brussels","continent":"Europe","missing":"Hundreds of beers, each poured in its own glass, medieval cities a short train ride apart, and fries done properly.","dontMiss":["Bruges’s canals early in the morning","The van Eyck brothers’ Ghent Altarpiece","A Trappist beer at the café beside Westvleteren abbey"],"lookFor":"Bluebells carpeting the beech forest of Hallerbos, south of Brussels, usually in mid to late April.","eat":"Moules-frites: a pot of mussels steamed with celery, onion and white wine, and fries on the side.","fact":"Belgium spent 541 days without a new government after its 2010 election, a world record at the time."},
  "BF": {"name":"Burkina Faso","capital":"Ouagadougou","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Africa’s biggest film festival, hand-painted mud houses, and live music most nights.","dontMiss":["FESPACO, the pan-African film festival, in Ouagadougou","The painted Kassena houses of Tiébélé","The Sindou Peaks, rock needles in the southwest"],"lookFor":"Sacred crocodiles at the ponds of Sabou and Bazoulé, fed and protected by the villagers.","eat":"Tô: a stiff millet or maize porridge, eaten with okra or baobab-leaf sauce.","fact":"FESPACO, held in Ouagadougou every two years since 1969, is Africa’s largest film festival."},
  "BG": {"name":"Bulgaria","capital":"Sofia","continent":"Europe","missing":"Monasteries in mountain valleys, Roman ruins under city streets, and a Black Sea coast with cheap, good food.","dontMiss":["Rila Monastery, and the Seven Rila Lakes hike","Plovdiv’s Old Town and Roman theatre","Veliko Tarnovo and the Tsarevets fortress"],"lookFor":"Damask roses in bloom in the Rose Valley around Kazanlak, picked at dawn in late May and June.","eat":"Shopska salad: tomato, cucumber, peppers and grated sirene cheese, usually with a rakia.","fact":"Bulgarians traditionally nod to mean no and shake their heads to mean yes."},
  "BH": {"name":"Bahrain","capital":"Manama","continent":"Asia","missing":"Four thousand years of history on one small island: Dilmun burial mounds, pearl merchants’ houses and a seaside fort.","dontMiss":["Qal’at al-Bahrain, the fort on the ancient Dilmun capital","The Pearling Path through old Muharraq","The royal burial mounds at A’ali"],"lookFor":"The Tree of Life, a lone mesquite thought to be about 400 years old, growing in open desert.","eat":"Machboos: spiced rice cooked with chicken or fish, dried lime and baharat.","fact":"Bahrain means “two seas” in Arabic, possibly for the freshwater springs that rise under the salty Gulf."},
  "BI": {"name":"Burundi","capital":"Gitega","continent":"Africa","missing":"Beaches on Lake Tanganyika, hills of tea, and royal drummers keeping up a tradition centuries old.","dontMiss":["A drumming performance at the Gishora sanctuary","Saga Beach on Lake Tanganyika near Bujumbura","Kibira National Park’s rainforest and tea estates"],"lookFor":"Hippos and crocodiles in the Rusizi River delta, where it enters Lake Tanganyika near Bujumbura.","eat":"Grilled mukeke, a perch found only in Lake Tanganyika, with plantains or rice.","fact":"The ritual dance of Burundi’s royal drum is on UNESCO’s Intangible Heritage list; drums once stood for the king."},
  "BJ": {"name":"Benin","capital":"Porto-Novo","continent":"Africa","missing":"The birthplace of vodun, a village built on stilts in a lake, and the royal palaces of the old Dahomey kingdom.","dontMiss":["Ganvié, the stilt village on Lake Nokoué","The Royal Palaces of Abomey","Ouidah’s Route des Esclaves and Door of No Return"],"lookFor":"Royal pythons at Ouidah’s Temple of Pythons, sacred in vodun and often draped on visitors.","eat":"Amiwo: maize dough cooked in tomato and palm oil, usually served with chicken.","fact":"Benin has a public holiday for vodun, on 10 January, celebrated hardest on the beach at Ouidah."},
  "BM": {"name":"Bermuda","capital":"Hamilton","continent":"North America","missing":"Pink-sand beaches, a 17th-century town that’s a World Heritage Site, and shipwrecks on the reefs all around.","dontMiss":["The pink sand of Horseshoe Bay","St George’s, the old town","The Crystal Caves"],"lookFor":"Longtails, white-tailed tropicbirds, soaring over the south shore cliffs from March to September.","eat":"Fish chowder laced with black rum and sherry pepper sauce, and a fish sandwich on raisin bread.","fact":"Bermuda’s white stepped roofs are built to catch rain, the main source of fresh water."},
  "BN": {"name":"Brunei","capital":"Bandar Seri Begawan","continent":"Asia","missing":"Rainforest reached by longboat and canopy walk, and a capital with a whole village on stilts in the river.","dontMiss":["A water-taxi ride through Kampong Ayer","The canopy walkway in Ulu Temburong National Park","Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque at dusk"],"lookFor":"Proboscis monkeys in the mangroves along the Brunei River, seen from a boat near dusk.","eat":"Ambuyat: sticky sago starch twirled on bamboo sticks and dipped in a sour fruit sauce.","fact":"The sultan’s palace, Istana Nurul Iman, has 1,788 rooms, the largest residential palace in the world."},
  "BO": {"name":"Bolivia","capital":"Sucre / La Paz","continent":"South America","missing":"The world’s largest salt flat, markets at 4,000 m, and La Paz’s cable cars running over the rooftops.","dontMiss":["A three-day jeep trip across the Salar de Uyuni","Lake Titicaca and Isla del Sol","La Paz from its Mi Teleférico cable cars"],"lookFor":"James’s and Andean flamingos feeding in the red water of Laguna Colorada, on the Uyuni circuit.","eat":"Salteñas: baked pastries full of a juicy, slightly sweet stew, eaten mid-morning.","fact":"The Salar de Uyuni varies less than a metre in height across 10,000 km², so it’s used to calibrate satellites."},
  "BR": {"name":"Brazil","capital":"Brasília","continent":"South America","missing":"The Amazon, the Pantanal’s wildlife, Rio’s beaches between granite peaks, and food that changes at every state line.","dontMiss":["Rio from the top of Sugarloaf at sunset","Iguaçu Falls from the Brazilian side’s trail","The Pantanal, for jaguars and giant otters"],"lookFor":"Jaguars on the riverbanks near Porto Jofre in the northern Pantanal, best from July to October.","eat":"Feijoada: black beans stewed with pork, served with rice, farofa, greens and orange slices.","fact":"Brazil borders every South American country except Chile and Ecuador."},
  "BS": {"name":"Bahamas","capital":"Nassau","continent":"North America","missing":"Water so clear you can see the bottom from the plane, across hundreds of islands most visitors never leave Nassau for.","dontMiss":["The Exuma Cays by boat, sandbars and all","Harbour Island’s pink-sand beach","Diving the blue holes and barrier reef off Andros"],"lookFor":"West Indian flamingos, the national bird, by the tens of thousands on Great Inagua’s salt ponds.","eat":"Conch salad: raw conch diced with lime, onion, sweet peppers and hot chilli, made to order.","fact":"Columbus’s first landfall in the Americas, in 1492, was in the Bahamas, probably on San Salvador."},
  "BT": {"name":"Bhutan","capital":"Thimphu","continent":"Asia","missing":"Monasteries clinging to cliffs, prayer flags on every pass, and a country that limits tourism on purpose.","dontMiss":["The hike up to Tiger’s Nest monastery above Paro","Punakha Dzong, where two rivers meet","A tsechu festival with masked dances"],"lookFor":"Black-necked cranes wintering in the Phobjikha Valley, from about November to February.","eat":"Ema datshi: green chillies cooked in cheese, eaten with red rice.","fact":"Bhutan absorbs more carbon than it emits, and its constitution requires 60 percent of the land to stay forested."},
  "BW": {"name":"Botswana","capital":"Gaborone","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Okavango Delta by dugout canoe, elephants by the thousand along the Chobe, and the silence of Kalahari nights.","dontMiss":["A mokoro trip through the Okavango Delta","A sunset boat on the Chobe River","The Makgadikgadi Pans and their meerkats"],"lookFor":"Elephants coming down to drink along the Chobe River in the dry season, roughly June to October.","eat":"Seswaa: beef boiled for hours and pounded tender, served with pap.","fact":"The Okavango is a river that never reaches the sea; it spreads out and evaporates into the Kalahari."},
  "BY": {"name":"Belarus","capital":"Minsk","continent":"Europe","missing":"Travel there is complicated right now, but it has Europe’s oldest lowland forest, bison and all, and Minsk’s grand Soviet avenues.","dontMiss":["Belovezhskaya Pushcha National Park and its old oaks","Mir Castle and Nesvizh Palace, both UNESCO sites","Minsk’s Independence Avenue and its metro stations"],"lookFor":"European bison in Belovezhskaya Pushcha, the forest that helped save them from extinction.","eat":"Draniki: grated potato pancakes, fried crisp and served with sour cream.","fact":"The world’s largest dump truck, the BelAZ 75710, is built in Belarus; it carries about 450 tonnes."},
  "BZ": {"name":"Belize","capital":"Belmopan","continent":"North America","missing":"A barrier reef just offshore, Maya temples in the jungle, and howler monkeys as your alarm clock.","dontMiss":["Snorkeling Hol Chan and Shark Ray Alley","The ATM cave and its Maya skeletons","Climbing the main temple at Caracol or Xunantunich"],"lookFor":"Black howler monkeys, called “baboons” locally, at the Community Baboon Sanctuary near Bermudian Landing.","eat":"Rice and beans cooked in coconut milk, with stewed chicken and potato salad.","fact":"Belize’s Cockscomb Basin was the world’s first protected area set up for jaguars."},
  "CA": {"name":"Canada","capital":"Ottawa","continent":"North America","missing":"Wilderness on a scale that’s hard to picture, plus Montreal’s bagels and Quebec City’s winter carnival.","dontMiss":["The Icefields Parkway from Lake Louise to Jasper","Old Quebec City, ideally in winter","Tofino and Vancouver Island’s west coast beaches"],"lookFor":"Polar bears waiting for the sea ice near Churchill, Manitoba, in October and November.","eat":"Poutine: fries and squeaky cheese curds under hot gravy, from Quebec.","fact":"Canada has the longest coastline of any country, about 243,000 km counting its islands."},
  "CD": {"name":"DR Congo","capital":"Kinshasa","continent":"Africa","missing":"The east isn’t safe right now, but Virunga’s gorillas, Nyiragongo’s lava lake and Kinshasa’s rumba are worth waiting for.","dontMiss":["Mountain gorillas in Virunga National Park","A night on Nyiragongo’s rim above the lava lake","Live Congolese rumba in a Kinshasa bar"],"lookFor":"Bonobos, found in the wild only in this country, at the Lola ya Bonobo sanctuary outside Kinshasa.","eat":"Moambe chicken: chicken in a palm-nut sauce, with fufu or rice.","fact":"The Congo is the world’s deepest river, measured at more than 220 m in places."},
  "CF": {"name":"Central African Republic","capital":"Bangui","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: a forest clearing where elephants gather by the dozen, and the BaAka’s forest knowledge.","dontMiss":["Dzanga Bai, where forest elephants dig for minerals","Tracking habituated lowland gorillas at Bai Hokou","Boali Falls, northwest of Bangui"],"lookFor":"Forest elephants, smaller than savanna ones, at Dzanga Bai in the Dzanga-Sangha reserve.","eat":"Gozo, a cassava dough, with ngunza, cassava leaves stewed with peanut or palm oil.","fact":"The polyphonic singing of the Aka people of the southwestern forests is on UNESCO’s Intangible Heritage list."},
  "CG": {"name":"Congo","capital":"Brazzaville","continent":"Africa","missing":"Some of Africa’s least-visited rainforest, with gorillas and forest elephants, and Brazzaville across the river from Kinshasa.","dontMiss":["Odzala-Kokoua National Park, for lowland gorillas","The Congo River rapids at the edge of Brazzaville","The beaches and fishing boats of Pointe-Noire"],"lookFor":"Western lowland gorillas in Odzala-Kokoua National Park, tracked on foot with guides.","eat":"Saka-saka: pounded cassava leaves cooked with palm oil and fish, with chikwangue.","fact":"Brazzaville and Kinshasa face each other across the Congo River, the closest capitals after Rome and the Vatican."},
  "CH": {"name":"Switzerland","capital":"Bern","continent":"Europe","missing":"Trains that climb mountains to the minute, lakes and rivers clean enough to swim in town, and Alpine villages with no cars.","dontMiss":["Zermatt and the Matterhorn","The Lauterbrunnen valley and its waterfalls","A summer float down the Aare in Bern"],"lookFor":"Alpine ibex on the slopes of Piz Albris above Pontresina, sometimes near the village in spring.","eat":"Fondue and raclette in winter, and rösti, crisp fried grated potato, all year.","fact":"Switzerland has no single head of state; the seven-member Federal Council shares the role."},
  "CI": {"name":"Côte d’Ivoire","capital":"Yamoussoukro","continent":"Africa","missing":"Abidjan’s lagoon skyline and nightlife, Grand-Bassam’s colonial streets, and a basilica modeled on St Peter’s.","dontMiss":["Grand-Bassam’s old colonial quarter","The Basilica of Our Lady of Peace in Yamoussoukro","Taï National Park’s rainforest"],"lookFor":"Chimpanzees cracking nuts with stone and wooden hammers in Taï National Park.","eat":"Attiéké: fermented cassava couscous, with grilled fish, chilli and raw onion.","fact":"Côte d’Ivoire grows about 40 percent of the world’s cocoa, more than any other country."},
  "CL": {"name":"Chile","capital":"Santiago","continent":"South America","missing":"A country over 4,000 km long: the driest desert, Patagonian granite towers, and vineyards an hour from the capital.","dontMiss":["The W Trek in Torres del Paine","Stargazing in the Atacama around San Pedro","Valparaíso’s hillside lifts and painted streets"],"lookFor":"Guanacos grazing all over Torres del Paine, often right beside the road.","eat":"Pastel de choclo: ground sweet corn baked over beef, chicken, olives and egg, in summer.","fact":"The Atacama is so like Mars that NASA tests its rovers there."},
  "CM": {"name":"Cameroon","capital":"Yaoundé","continent":"Africa","missing":"Called “Africa in miniature” for good reason: rainforest, savanna, a volcano and black-sand beaches in one country.","dontMiss":["Climbing Mount Cameroon from Buea","The black-sand beaches of Limbe","The sultan’s palace and museum in Foumban"],"lookFor":"Drills, a rare forest monkey found almost only here, in the rescue groups at the Limbe Wildlife Centre.","eat":"Ndolé: bitterleaf stewed with ground peanuts and shrimp or beef, with fried plantains.","fact":"Cameroon has two official languages, French and English, and more than 250 local ones."},
  "CN": {"name":"China","capital":"Beijing","continent":"Asia","missing":"Food that changes every few hundred kilometres, karst rivers, and walls and temples measured in millennia.","dontMiss":["A wild stretch of the Great Wall, like Jiankou","The Li River between Guilin and Yangshuo","Xi’an’s Terracotta Army and Muslim Quarter"],"lookFor":"Giant pandas at the Chengdu Research Base, most active around morning feeding time.","eat":"Peking duck, carved at the table and wrapped in thin pancakes with scallion and sweet bean sauce.","fact":"China spans five geographical time zones but runs on one, Beijing time."},
  "CO": {"name":"Colombia","capital":"Bogotá","continent":"South America","missing":"Caribbean old towns, coffee country in the Andes, and salsa in Cali that everyone seems to know how to dance.","dontMiss":["Cartagena’s walled city in the evening","The four-day trek to Ciudad Perdida","Medellín’s Comuna 13 and its outdoor escalators"],"lookFor":"Quindío wax palms, the national tree and the world’s tallest palm, in the Cocora Valley near Salento.","eat":"Bandeja paisa: beans, rice, chicharrón, chorizo, egg, arepa and avocado on one plate.","fact":"Colombia has recorded more bird species than any other country, over 1,900."},
  "CR": {"name":"Costa Rica","capital":"San José","continent":"North America","missing":"Cloud forest, two coasts a few hours apart, and about a quarter of the country protected as parks and reserves.","dontMiss":["The hanging bridges in the Monteverde cloud forest","Corcovado National Park on the Osa Peninsula","Arenal Volcano and the hot rivers around it"],"lookFor":"Resplendent quetzals around San Gerardo de Dota, best from February to May.","eat":"Gallo pinto: rice and black beans fried with onion and Salsa Lizano, for breakfast.","fact":"Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948 and put the money into schools and health care."},
  "CU": {"name":"Cuba","capital":"Havana","continent":"North America","missing":"Havana’s crumbling colonnades, son music in Trinidad’s squares, and tobacco fields in the Viñales valley.","dontMiss":["Old Havana, then the Malecón at sunset","The tobacco farms and mogotes of Viñales","Trinidad’s cobbled center and its live music"],"lookFor":"The bee hummingbird, the world’s smallest bird, in the Zapata Swamp, with a local guide.","eat":"Ropa vieja: shredded beef stewed in tomato, with rice, black beans and fried plantains.","fact":"Hemingway lived outside Havana for about twenty years; his house, Finca Vigía, is kept as he left it."},
  "CV": {"name":"Cabo Verde","capital":"Praia","continent":"Africa","missing":"Ten volcanic islands, each different: hiking valleys on Santo Antão, morna on São Vicente, a crater village on Fogo.","dontMiss":["Hiking Santo Antão’s green valleys down to the sea","A night of live morna in Mindelo","Climbing Pico do Fogo from Chã das Caldeiras"],"lookFor":"Loggerhead turtles nesting on the beaches of Boa Vista and Sal, from about July to October.","eat":"Cachupa: a slow stew of hominy, beans and fish or meat, fried up for breakfast the next day.","fact":"The islands were uninhabited until Portuguese sailors arrived around 1456."},
  "CW": {"name":"Curaçao","capital":"Willemstad","continent":"North America","missing":"Willemstad’s painted Dutch waterfront, coves with clear water, and a countryside covered in cactus.","dontMiss":["The Handelskade and the floating Queen Emma Bridge","The coves of Playa Kenepa","Christoffel National Park"],"lookFor":"Flamingos feeding in the salt pans at Jan Kok.","eat":"Stoba, a slow stew of goat or beef, with funchi, a firm cornmeal like polenta.","fact":"Curaçao’s Mikvé Israel-Emanuel synagogue, from 1732, is the oldest in continuous use in the Americas."},
  "CY": {"name":"Cyprus","capital":"Nicosia","continent":"Europe","missing":"Byzantine churches painted wall to wall in the mountains, Roman mosaics by the sea, and village tavernas.","dontMiss":["The painted churches of the Troodos Mountains","The mosaics of Kato Paphos Archaeological Park","Crossing the Green Line on foot in Nicosia"],"lookFor":"Greater flamingos on Larnaca Salt Lake, from about November to March.","eat":"Grilled halloumi, as part of a full meze that can run to twenty small plates.","fact":"Nicosia is the world’s last divided capital, split by a UN buffer zone since 1974."},
  "CZ": {"name":"Czechia","capital":"Prague","continent":"Europe","missing":"Gothic and baroque towns that came through the 20th century intact, castles everywhere, and pilsner fresh from the tank.","dontMiss":["Prague’s Charles Bridge at dawn","Český Krumlov and its castle above the river","The sandstone gorges of Bohemian Switzerland"],"lookFor":"Carp hauled from the Třeboň fishponds in the autumn harvest, as they have been for centuries.","eat":"Svíčková: braised beef in a creamy root-vegetable sauce, with bread dumplings and cranberries.","fact":"Czechs drink more beer per person than anyone else, and have for about three decades."},
  "DE": {"name":"Germany","capital":"Berlin","continent":"Europe","missing":"Beer gardens, Christmas markets, castles over the Rhine, and Berlin’s museums and nightlife.","dontMiss":["Museum Island and the Wall memorial in Berlin","A Rhine valley cruise past the castles","Bamberg’s smoked beer and old town"],"lookFor":"White storks nesting on rooftops in Rühstädt, Brandenburg’s “stork village”, from April to August.","eat":"Currywurst in Berlin, or Schweinshaxe, roast pork knuckle with crackling, in Bavaria.","fact":"Long stretches of Germany’s autobahns still have no general speed limit."},
  "DJ": {"name":"Djibouti","capital":"Djibouti","continent":"Africa","missing":"Swimming with whale sharks, a salt lake far below sea level, and lava fields that look like another planet.","dontMiss":["Swimming with whale sharks in the Gulf of Tadjoura","Lake Assal’s salt flats, 155 m below sea level","Lac Abbé’s limestone chimneys at sunrise"],"lookFor":"Whale sharks in the Gulf of Tadjoura near Arta, from about November to January.","eat":"Skoudehkaris: rice and lamb cooked with tomato and spices, the national dish.","fact":"Lake Assal is the lowest point in Africa and one of the saltiest bodies of water on Earth."},
  "DK": {"name":"Denmark","capital":"Copenhagen","continent":"Europe","missing":"Copenhagen by bike, open-faced rye sandwiches, and Viking ships raised from the bottom of a fjord.","dontMiss":["Cycling Copenhagen, with a swim in the harbour baths","Skagen, where two seas meet at the tip","The Viking Ship Museum in Roskilde"],"lookFor":"Starling murmurations, called “black sun”, over the Wadden Sea marshes in March–April and September–October.","eat":"Smørrebrød: dense rye bread topped with herring, roast pork, or egg and shrimp.","fact":"Denmark’s flag, the Dannebrog, is the oldest national flag still in continuous use."},
  "DM": {"name":"Dominica","capital":"Roseau","continent":"North America","missing":"A rainforest island of waterfalls, hot springs and a boiling lake, with black-sand beaches and no big resorts.","dontMiss":["The all-day hike to the Boiling Lake","Swimming through Titou Gorge","Champagne Reef, where volcanic bubbles rise"],"lookFor":"Sperm whales, which live off the west coast year-round; best seen from November to March.","eat":"Callaloo soup of dasheen leaves, often with crab, made the national dish in 2013.","fact":"Dominica’s Boiling Lake is the second-largest hot lake in the world, after Frying Pan Lake in New Zealand."},
  "DO": {"name":"Dominican Republic","capital":"Santo Domingo","continent":"North America","missing":"The oldest European city in the Americas, cool mountain towns, and humpback whales calving in a bay every winter.","dontMiss":["Santo Domingo’s Zona Colonial","Los Haitises National Park by boat","Hiking Pico Duarte, the Caribbean’s highest peak"],"lookFor":"Humpback whales breeding and calving in Samaná Bay, from January to March.","eat":"La bandera: rice, red beans and stewed meat, the everyday lunch.","fact":"Santo Domingo’s cathedral, finished in 1540, is the oldest in the Americas."},
  "DZ": {"name":"Algeria","capital":"Algiers","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Sahara at its most varied, from rock-art plateaus to sand seas, plus Roman cities with almost no one in them.","dontMiss":["The rock art and sandstone forests of Tassili n’Ajjer","The Roman cities of Djémila and Timgad","The M’zab valley’s hilltop towns around Ghardaïa"],"lookFor":"Barbary macaques along the Chiffa Gorge near Blida, at the spot called the Ruisseau des Singes.","eat":"Couscous steamed over a stew of lamb and vegetables; many families make it for Friday lunch.","fact":"Algeria is Africa’s largest country, and more than four-fifths of it is Sahara."},
  "EC": {"name":"Ecuador","capital":"Quito","continent":"South America","missing":"The Galápagos, and on the mainland, Andean markets, cloud forest an hour from Quito, and the Amazon.","dontMiss":["The Galápagos, ideally on a small boat","Otavalo’s Saturday market","The Quilotoa crater lake loop"],"lookFor":"Marine iguanas, the only lizards that feed in the sea, piled up on Galápagos lava rock.","eat":"Ecuadorian ceviche: shrimp in a tangy citrus and tomato broth, with toasted corn or plantain chips.","fact":"Because Earth bulges at the equator, Chimborazo’s summit is the farthest point from Earth’s center."},
  "EE": {"name":"Estonia","capital":"Tallinn","continent":"Europe","missing":"Tallinn’s medieval walls, bog boardwalks at dawn, and a sauna culture taken very seriously.","dontMiss":["Tallinn’s Old Town, walls and towers","The bog boardwalk at Viru Raba in Lahemaa","A smoke sauna in Võrumaa"],"lookFor":"Brown bears seen from forest hides in Alutaguse, in the northeast, from late spring to summer.","eat":"Dark rye bread with everything, often topped with Baltic sprats and egg.","fact":"Estonia was the first country to let everyone vote online, starting with its 2005 local elections."},
  "EG": {"name":"Egypt","capital":"Cairo","continent":"Africa","missing":"Temples, tombs and the Nile at felucca pace, plus Red Sea reefs a short drive from the desert.","dontMiss":["The Giza pyramids and the Grand Egyptian Museum","The Valley of the Kings and Karnak at Luxor","Abu Simbel at sunrise"],"lookFor":"Coral gardens thick with reef fish at Ras Mohammed National Park, off Sharm el-Sheikh.","eat":"Koshari: rice, lentils, pasta and chickpeas under spiced tomato sauce and crisp fried onions.","fact":"Cleopatra lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid."},
  "ER": {"name":"Eritrea","capital":"Asmara","continent":"Africa","missing":"Permits take patience, but Asmara’s 1930s Italian streets and espresso bars are unlike anywhere else in Africa.","dontMiss":["Asmara’s modernist buildings, like the Fiat Tagliero","The old railway down the escarpment from Asmara","Massawa’s Ottoman-era old town on the Red Sea"],"lookFor":"Beles, prickly-pear fruit, sold from roadside cactus in the highlands from about July to September.","eat":"Zigni, a spicy berbere stew, on injera, followed by a macchiato.","fact":"Asmara was made a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017 for its early 20th-century modernist architecture."},
  "ES": {"name":"Spain","capital":"Madrid","continent":"Europe","missing":"Late dinners and later nights, Moorish palaces in the south, and a different food culture every few hours on the train.","dontMiss":["The Alhambra in Granada, booked ahead","Gaudí’s Sagrada Família in Barcelona","The pintxos bars of San Sebastián"],"lookFor":"Griffon vultures circling the cliffs at Monfragüe National Park; Spain has most of Europe’s vultures.","eat":"Tortilla española, a thick potato omelette eaten by the slice, and jamón ibérico.","fact":"Spain runs on Central European Time, an hour ahead of the sun, since Franco aligned it with Germany in 1940."},
  "ET": {"name":"Ethiopia","capital":"Addis Ababa","continent":"Africa","missing":"Check which regions are open, but rock-cut churches, its own calendar and the coffee ceremony are worth the planning.","dontMiss":["Lalibela’s rock-hewn churches","The Simien Mountains and their gelada troops","The sulfur springs of Dallol in the Danakil"],"lookFor":"Gelada monkeys, found only in Ethiopia, grazing in huge troops along the Simien Mountains’ cliff edges.","eat":"Injera with doro wat, a slow chicken stew rich with berbere and boiled eggs.","fact":"Ethiopia uses its own calendar, which runs about seven to eight years behind the Gregorian one."},
  "FI": {"name":"Finland","capital":"Helsinki","continent":"Europe","missing":"A lakeside sauna followed by a swim, the summer midnight sun, and northern lights over Lapland.","dontMiss":["A lakeside sauna and a cold plunge","The northern lights in Lapland, September to March","Suomenlinna sea fortress by ferry from Helsinki"],"lookFor":"Reindeer, herded but free-ranging, wandering across the roads of Lapland all year.","eat":"Karelian pies: rye crusts filled with rice porridge, topped with egg butter.","fact":"Finland has about three million saunas for about 5.6 million people."},
  "FJ": {"name":"Fiji","capital":"Suva","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Reef-ringed islands, villages that welcome you with a bowl of kava, and soft corals divers travel far to see.","dontMiss":["Island hopping in the Yasawas","Diving the soft corals of the Somosomo Strait","A village visit with a kava ceremony"],"lookFor":"Manta rays feeding in the channels of the Yasawa Islands, from about May to October.","eat":"Kokoda: raw fish cured in lime and coconut milk with chilli.","fact":"The 180° meridian runs across Fiji’s Taveuni island; the date line itself bends around the country."},
  "FM": {"name":"Micronesia","capital":"Palikir","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Four island states, four cultures: basalt ruins on Pohnpei, stone money on Yap, and a lagoon full of sunken warships at Chuuk.","dontMiss":["Nan Madol, the basalt-log city off Pohnpei","Wreck diving in Chuuk Lagoon","Yap’s stone money banks"],"lookFor":"Manta rays at the cleaning stations in Yap’s channels, there all year round.","eat":"Breadfruit and reef fish, and on Pohnpei, sakau, a strong kava pounded on stones and shared after dark.","fact":"Yap’s stone money discs can be 3 m across, and they change owners without ever being moved."},
  "FO": {"name":"Faroe Islands","capital":"Tórshavn","continent":"Europe","missing":"Eighteen steep green islands in the North Atlantic, villages under grass roofs, and cliffs full of seabirds.","dontMiss":["Saksun and its grass-roofed church","Gásadalur’s waterfall dropping straight into the sea","The walk to Lake Sørvágsvatn above the ocean cliffs"],"lookFor":"Puffins nesting on Mykines, from about May to August.","eat":"Skerpikjøt, wind-dried mutton hung in slatted sheds, and fresh North Atlantic fish.","fact":"Sheep outnumber people on the Faroes, and the islands’ name probably means “sheep islands”."},
  "FR": {"name":"France","capital":"Paris","continent":"Europe","missing":"Lunch as a sit-down affair, a cheese for every region, and village markets that are the event of the week.","dontMiss":["The Musée d’Orsay, then a walk along the Seine","Mont-Saint-Michel at high tide","A Provence village market in summer"],"lookFor":"Greater flamingos in the lagoons of the Camargue, in the Rhône delta, all year.","eat":"Boeuf bourguignon: beef braised in red Burgundy with mushrooms, onions and lardons.","fact":"France spans 12 time zones thanks to its overseas territories, more than any other country."},
  "GA": {"name":"Gabon","capital":"Libreville","continent":"Africa","missing":"A country nearly 90 percent forest, where elephants, hippos and gorillas wander onto Atlantic beaches.","dontMiss":["Loango National Park, where forest meets beach","Lopé National Park’s savanna and forest","Leatherbacks nesting at Pongara near Libreville"],"lookFor":"Mandrills, in groups of hundreds, in Lopé National Park.","eat":"Poulet nyembwe: chicken in a palm-nut sauce, with rice or cassava.","fact":"Natural nuclear reactors ran underground at Oklo, in Gabon, about 2 billion years ago."},
  "GB": {"name":"United Kingdom","capital":"London","continent":"Europe","missing":"Old pubs and older cathedrals, coastal walks, and London’s free museums, with somewhere quite different an hour away.","dontMiss":["The British Museum and Tate Modern in London, both free","Edinburgh’s Old Town and the climb up Arthur’s Seat","The Scottish Highlands or the Lake District"],"lookFor":"Bluebell woods in late April and May; Britain has around half the world’s bluebells.","eat":"A Sunday roast with Yorkshire puddings, and fish and chips eaten by the sea.","fact":"Nowhere in the UK is more than about 70 miles (113 km) from the sea."},
  "GD": {"name":"Grenada","capital":"St. George’s","continent":"North America","missing":"The Spice Isle: nutmeg drying in the sun, waterfalls in the rainforest, and an underwater sculpture park off the beach.","dontMiss":["Snorkeling the Underwater Sculpture Park at Molinere Bay","Grand Anse beach","The nutmeg processing station in Gouyave"],"lookFor":"Mona monkeys, brought from West Africa centuries ago, in the forest around Grand Etang Lake.","eat":"Oil down: breadfruit, salted meat, dumplings and callaloo cooked down in coconut milk.","fact":"There’s a nutmeg on Grenada’s flag; the island has long been one of the world’s top producers."},
  "GE": {"name":"Georgia","capital":"Tbilisi","continent":"Asia","missing":"Wine fermented in clay jars buried in the ground, supras with endless toasts, and mountain villages with medieval towers.","dontMiss":["Gergeti Trinity Church below Mount Kazbek","The stone towers of Ushguli in Svaneti","Tbilisi’s sulfur baths and old town"],"lookFor":"Saperavi grapes ripening in Kakheti’s vineyards before the rtveli harvest in September.","eat":"Khachapuri, especially the Adjarian boat with an egg and butter in the middle.","fact":"Traces of winemaking in Georgia go back to about 6000 BC, among the oldest known anywhere."},
  "GH": {"name":"Ghana","capital":"Accra","continent":"Africa","missing":"Highlife music, the castles of the Cape Coast, a canopy walk in rainforest, and street food at all hours.","dontMiss":["Cape Coast and Elmina castles","The canopy walkway at Kakum National Park","A walking safari at Mole National Park"],"lookFor":"Elephants at the watering holes below the lodge at Mole National Park, in the dry season.","eat":"Jollof rice, cooked with tomato and pepper, and waakye from a street stall.","fact":"Lake Volta, created by the Akosombo Dam, is the world’s largest reservoir by surface area."},
  "GL": {"name":"Greenland","capital":"Nuuk","continent":"North America","missing":"Icebergs the size of towns drifting past painted houses, dog sleds in winter, and the midnight sun in summer.","dontMiss":["Ilulissat Icefjord","Dog-sledding around Ilulissat or Sisimiut in late winter","Kayaking or sailing among the icebergs of Disko Bay"],"lookFor":"Musk oxen on the tundra around Kangerlussuaq, all year.","eat":"Suaasat, a soup of seal, whale or caribou with rice and onion.","fact":"Greenland’s ice sheet is up to 3 km thick and holds enough water to raise sea levels about 7 m."},
  "GM": {"name":"Gambia","capital":"Banjul","continent":"Africa","missing":"Africa’s smallest mainland country: a slow river trip, cheap birdwatching, and Atlantic beaches.","dontMiss":["A boat trip up the Gambia River to Janjanbureh","Kunta Kinteh Island, a UNESCO slave-trade site","Abuko Nature Reserve near the coast"],"lookFor":"The crocodiles of Kachikally pool in Bakau, a sacred site where women pray for fertility.","eat":"Domoda: a peanut-butter stew with meat or fish, over rice.","fact":"Gambia is surrounded on every side by Senegal, apart from its short Atlantic coast."},
  "GN": {"name":"Guinea","capital":"Conakry","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Fouta Djallon highlands’ waterfalls and villages, and the hills where the Niger and Senegal rivers begin.","dontMiss":["Hiking between waterfalls in the Fouta Djallon","The Îles de Los by boat from Conakry","The Voile de la Mariée waterfall near Kindia"],"lookFor":"The small chimpanzee group at Bossou, famous for cracking nuts with stone tools.","eat":"Rice with sauce feuille: cassava or sweet-potato leaves cooked with palm oil and fish or meat.","fact":"Guinea holds about a quarter of the world’s known bauxite reserves, the ore aluminium comes from."},
  "GQ": {"name":"Equatorial Guinea","capital":"Malabo / Ciudad de la Paz","continent":"Africa","missing":"Bioko island’s volcanic rainforest and rare monkeys, and remote beaches where four kinds of sea turtle nest.","dontMiss":["The turtle-nesting beaches near Ureca on Bioko","Malabo’s cathedral and colonial old town","Monte Alén National Park on the mainland"],"lookFor":"Leatherback and green turtles nesting on Bioko’s southern beaches, from about November to February.","eat":"Pepesoup: a spicy broth of fish or meat with chilli and local herbs.","fact":"It’s the only country in Africa with Spanish as an official language."},
  "GR": {"name":"Greece","capital":"Athens","continent":"Europe","missing":"Islands with distinct personalities, ancient theatres you can still sit in, and long lunches by the water.","dontMiss":["The Acropolis right when it opens","Meteora’s monasteries on rock pillars","Walking the Samaria Gorge on Crete"],"lookFor":"Loggerhead turtles nesting on the beaches of Laganas Bay, Zakynthos, from about June to August.","eat":"Grilled octopus at a harbour taverna, or a horiatiki salad with a slab of feta on top.","fact":"Greece has about 6,000 islands and islets, but only around 230 are inhabited."},
  "GT": {"name":"Guatemala","capital":"Guatemala City","continent":"North America","missing":"Maya temples above the jungle canopy, a lake ringed by volcanoes, and markets where Maya languages are spoken.","dontMiss":["Tikal’s temples at sunrise","Lake Atitlán and its villages","Antigua’s cobbled streets below Volcán de Agua"],"lookFor":"Ocellated turkeys strutting around Tikal’s plazas, and howler monkeys roaring at dawn.","eat":"Pepián: a thick stew of chicken or beef in a sauce of roasted seeds, tomatoes and chillies.","fact":"Tikal’s temples stood in for a rebel base in the first Star Wars film, in 1977."},
  "GU": {"name":"Guam","capital":"Hagåtña","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Chamorro culture going back thousands of years, latte stone pillars, and warm reefs a few steps off the beach.","dontMiss":["Two Lovers Point above Tumon Bay","The Spanish forts and bridges around Umatac","The beach and caves at Ritidian Point"],"lookFor":"Green and hawksbill turtles grazing over the reefs at Tumon and Piti.","eat":"Red rice coloured with achote seeds, and chicken kelaguen, chopped chicken with lemon, coconut and chilli.","fact":"Most of Guam’s native forest birds vanished after the brown tree snake arrived, around the end of WWII."},
  "GW": {"name":"Guinea-Bissau","capital":"Bissau","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Bijagós archipelago: 88 islands of mangroves, sacred forests, and hippos that swim in the sea.","dontMiss":["The Bijagós islands by boat from Bissau","João Vieira and Poilão Marine National Park","Bissau Velho, the old colonial quarter"],"lookFor":"Saltwater hippos in the lagoons of Orango Island, in the Bijagós.","eat":"Caldo de mancarra: chicken in peanut sauce, over rice.","fact":"Poilão, a tiny Bijagós island, is one of the largest green turtle nesting sites in the Atlantic."},
  "GY": {"name":"Guyana","capital":"Georgetown","continent":"South America","missing":"Kaieteur Falls with almost nobody else there, Indigenous-run rainforest lodges, and Georgetown’s wooden cathedral.","dontMiss":["Kaieteur Falls, flying in and walking to the edge","The Rupununi savannas and their ranch lodges","The canopy walkway at Iwokrama"],"lookFor":"Golden rocket frogs living inside giant tank bromeliads near Kaieteur Falls.","eat":"Pepperpot: meat stewed dark with cassareep, cinnamon and hot peppers, at Christmas.","fact":"Kaieteur Falls has a single drop of 226 m, about four times the height of Niagara."},
  "HK": {"name":"Hong Kong","capital":"Hong Kong","continent":"Asia","missing":"Dim sum at 7 a.m., a harbour crossed by a ferry that’s run since the 1880s, and hiking trails ten minutes from the towers.","dontMiss":["The Star Ferry across Victoria Harbour","Victoria Peak, by the Peak Tram or on foot","The Dragon’s Back trail, or the Big Buddha on Lantau"],"lookFor":"Chinese white dolphins, which are actually pink, in the waters off Lantau.","eat":"Dim sum with tea, and wonton noodles or roast goose over rice.","fact":"The Central–Mid-Levels escalator, about 800 m long, is the longest outdoor covered escalator system in the world."},
  "HN": {"name":"Honduras","capital":"Tegucigalpa","continent":"North America","missing":"Copán’s carved stelae, cheap diving off Utila and Roatán, and cloud forests in the mountains.","dontMiss":["Copán’s Hieroglyphic Stairway","Diving the reefs off Utila or Roatán","Pico Bonito’s forests and rivers near La Ceiba"],"lookFor":"Scarlet macaws, the national bird, flying free over the Copán ruins.","eat":"Baleadas: thick flour tortillas folded over refried beans, cream and crumbled cheese.","fact":"Copán’s Hieroglyphic Stairway holds about 2,200 glyphs, the longest known Maya inscription."},
  "HR": {"name":"Croatia","capital":"Zagreb","continent":"Europe","missing":"Islands by ferry, a Roman palace people still live in, and seafood eaten where it was caught that morning.","dontMiss":["Plitvice Lakes’ boardwalks early in the morning","Diocletian’s Palace in Split","Dubrovnik’s city walls, before the cruise crowds"],"lookFor":"Griffon vultures on the island of Cres, where a rescue center in Beli looks after the colony.","eat":"Peka: lamb or octopus slow-baked under an iron bell covered in embers.","fact":"The necktie comes from Croatia: “cravat” derives from “Croat”, after 17th-century Croatian soldiers."},
  "HT": {"name":"Haiti","capital":"Port-au-Prince","continent":"North America","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: a mountaintop fortress, gingerbread houses, and an art scene like nowhere else.","dontMiss":["The Citadelle Laferrière above Milot","Jacmel’s ironwork balconies and Carnival masks","The pools and waterfalls of Bassin Bleu"],"lookFor":"The Hispaniolan trogon, the national bird, in the pine forests of La Visite National Park.","eat":"Griot: marinated pork, fried crisp, with pikliz, a spicy pickled slaw.","fact":"Haiti was the first nation founded by a successful slave revolt, declaring independence in 1804."},
  "HU": {"name":"Hungary","capital":"Budapest","continent":"Europe","missing":"Thermal baths at every temperature, Budapest’s ruin bars, and paprika in every pot.","dontMiss":["The Széchenyi or Gellért thermal baths","Budapest’s Parliament lit up, seen from the Danube","Lake Balaton and the Tihany peninsula"],"lookFor":"Tens of thousands of common cranes stopping at Hortobágy National Park in October.","eat":"Gulyás: beef and paprika soup cooked slowly, often in a kettle over a fire.","fact":"Lake Hévíz is the largest thermal lake in the world you can swim in, warm enough even in winter."},
  "ID": {"name":"Indonesia","capital":"Jakarta","continent":"Asia","missing":"Seventeen thousand islands: Bali’s temples, Komodo’s dragons, Java’s volcanoes, and coral reefs in between.","dontMiss":["Borobudur at sunrise","Komodo National Park and its dragons","Diving the reefs of Raja Ampat"],"lookFor":"Orangutans at the feeding stations of Tanjung Puting National Park, Borneo, reached by river boat.","eat":"Rendang: beef slow-cooked in coconut milk and spices until nearly dry, from West Sumatra.","fact":"Indonesia has more active volcanoes than any other country, about 130 of them."},
  "IE": {"name":"Ireland","capital":"Dublin","continent":"Europe","missing":"Pubs with live trad sessions, sea cliffs and empty green headlands in the west, and conversation as a pastime.","dontMiss":["The Cliffs of Moher, or the quieter Slieve League","The Slea Head Drive around the Dingle Peninsula","A trad session in a Doolin or Galway pub"],"lookFor":"Puffins nesting on Skellig Michael, off County Kerry, from May to early August.","eat":"Irish stew of lamb, potatoes and onions, with brown soda bread.","fact":"Ireland has no native snakes; after the last Ice Age they never made it back across the sea."},
  "IL": {"name":"Israel","capital":"Jerusalem (disputed)","continent":"Asia","missing":"Check the advisories first, but Jerusalem’s Old City, floating in the Dead Sea and Tel Aviv’s beaches are worth it.","dontMiss":["Jerusalem’s Old City and its four quarters","Masada at sunrise, then the Dead Sea","Tel Aviv’s Carmel Market and beach promenade"],"lookFor":"Tens of thousands of common cranes wintering in the Hula Valley, from about November to March.","eat":"Hummus, and falafel or sabich in pita with tahini and pickles.","fact":"The Dead Sea shore is the lowest land on Earth, about 430 m below sea level and still dropping."},
  "IN": {"name":"India","capital":"New Delhi","continent":"Asia","missing":"Every state with its own food and language, festivals all year, and forts, temples and tigers in one trip.","dontMiss":["The Taj Mahal at sunrise","Varanasi’s ghats and the evening aarti","Kerala’s backwaters on a houseboat"],"lookFor":"Bengal tigers in Ranthambore or Bandhavgarh National Park, easiest to spot from about March to May.","eat":"A thali: rice, breads, dals and vegetable dishes on one plate, different in every state.","fact":"India’s constitution recognizes 22 languages, and hundreds more are spoken across the country."},
  "IQ": {"name":"Iraq","capital":"Baghdad","continent":"Asia","missing":"Ur, Babylon and the southern marshes: the land where cities and writing began, slowly reopening to visitors.","dontMiss":["The ziggurat of Ur near Nasiriyah","A boat through the Mesopotamian Marshes","Erbil’s citadel, lived in for thousands of years"],"lookFor":"Water buffalo swimming between the reed islands of the Mesopotamian Marshes.","eat":"Masgouf: carp split open and grilled slowly beside a wood fire, a Baghdad tradition.","fact":"Cuneiform, the earliest known writing, was developed in southern Iraq, at Uruk, by about 3200 BC."},
  "IR": {"name":"Iran","capital":"Tehran","continent":"Asia","missing":"When travel is easier: Isfahan’s tiled domes, Persian gardens, and hospitality that turns a taxi ride into dinner.","dontMiss":["Isfahan’s Naqsh-e Jahan Square","The ruins of Persepolis near Shiraz","Yazd’s old town and its windcatchers"],"lookFor":"Saffron crocuses in flower in Khorasan’s fields in late autumn, picked by hand at dawn.","eat":"Chelow kebab: grilled lamb or chicken over buttered saffron rice, with a charred tomato.","fact":"Some of Iran’s qanats, underground water channels dug over 2,000 years ago, still carry water."},
  "IS": {"name":"Iceland","capital":"Reykjavík","continent":"Europe","missing":"Glaciers, black-sand beaches, hot pools in the middle of nowhere, and summer light that doesn’t quit.","dontMiss":["The Golden Circle: Þingvellir, Geysir and Gullfoss","Jökulsárlón’s icebergs and Diamond Beach","A soak in a town swimming pool, as locals do"],"lookFor":"Atlantic puffins on the sea cliffs at Látrabjarg or Borgarfjörður eystri, from May to August.","eat":"Kjötsúpa, a lamb and root-vegetable soup, with skyr for dessert.","fact":"Iceland’s parliament, the Althing, first met at Þingvellir in 930, one of the oldest in the world."},
  "IT": {"name":"Italy","capital":"Rome","continent":"Europe","missing":"Every town has a fresco or piazza worth the detour, and every region its own pasta and wine.","dontMiss":["Rome’s Pantheon and the Forum","Florence’s Uffizi, then the view from San Miniato","The Amalfi Coast or Cinque Terre on foot"],"lookFor":"Alpine ibex on the slopes of Gran Paradiso National Park, the park that saved them.","eat":"Pasta that changes by region: carbonara in Rome, ragù in Bologna, pesto in Genoa.","fact":"Italy has more UNESCO World Heritage Sites than any other country."},
  "JM": {"name":"Jamaica","capital":"Kingston","continent":"North America","missing":"Blue Mountain coffee where it grows, reggae in its own backyard, and jerk smoke along every roadside.","dontMiss":["Hiking Blue Mountain Peak for sunrise","The Bob Marley Museum in Kingston","Rafting the Rio Grande on bamboo"],"lookFor":"The doctor bird, a hummingbird with long streamer tail feathers, found only in Jamaica.","eat":"Ackee and saltfish, the national dish, with fried dumplings or roast breadfruit.","fact":"Jamaica’s bobsled team first competed at the 1988 Winter Olympics in Calgary."},
  "JO": {"name":"Jordan","capital":"Amman","continent":"Asia","missing":"Petra’s rock-cut city at dawn, a night in Wadi Rum’s desert, and floating in the Dead Sea.","dontMiss":["Petra, including the climb to the Monastery","A night in a Wadi Rum desert camp","The Roman city of Jerash"],"lookFor":"The black iris, the national flower, on hillsides around Madaba and in the north in March and April.","eat":"Mansaf: lamb cooked in a sauce of fermented dried yogurt, over rice, eaten from a shared tray.","fact":"Jordan is nearly landlocked; its only coast is about 26 km of Red Sea shore at Aqaba."},
  "JP": {"name":"Japan","capital":"Tokyo","continent":"Asia","missing":"Food that rewards every meal, trains on time to the second, and temples in quiet hillside towns.","dontMiss":["Kyoto’s temples early, before the tour buses","A night in a ryokan with its own hot spring","The Kumano Kodo pilgrimage trails"],"lookFor":"Snow monkeys bathing in hot springs at Jigokudani, in winter.","eat":"Ramen in its regional styles: tonkotsu in Fukuoka, miso in Sapporo.","fact":"A 2023 recount using digital maps found Japan has 14,125 islands, about double the old figure."},
  "KE": {"name":"Kenya","capital":"Nairobi","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Maasai Mara’s plains, Lamu’s car-free old town, and Mount Kenya’s high moorland.","dontMiss":["The Maasai Mara during the wildebeest migration","Amboseli’s elephants below Kilimanjaro","Lamu’s old town and a dhow trip"],"lookFor":"Wildebeest crossing the Mara River in the Maasai Mara, from about July to October.","eat":"Nyama choma: grilled goat or beef, with ugali and kachumbari.","fact":"Nairobi National Park lies inside the capital; lions and rhinos roam with skyscrapers in view."},
  "KG": {"name":"Kyrgyzstan","capital":"Bishkek","continent":"Asia","missing":"Horse country: yurts by alpine lakes, trekking without crowds, and a nomad culture still very much in use.","dontMiss":["A night in a yurt camp at Song-Kul lake","The trek to Altyn-Arashan’s hot springs","Karakol’s Sunday animal market"],"lookFor":"The walnut forests of Arslanbob, the largest in the world, at harvest in September and October.","eat":"Beshbarmak, and kumis, fermented mare’s milk, from a herder’s yurt in summer.","fact":"Over 90 percent of Kyrgyzstan is mountains."},
  "KH": {"name":"Cambodia","capital":"Phnom Penh","continent":"Asia","missing":"Temples swallowed by forest, Phnom Penh’s riverside, and cooking built on fish sauce, lemongrass and Kampot pepper.","dontMiss":["Sunrise at Angkor Wat, then Ta Prohm’s tree roots","Phnom Penh’s Royal Palace and Tuol Sleng museum","Kampot’s pepper farms and the crab market at Kep"],"lookFor":"Irrawaddy dolphins in the Mekong at Kampi, near Kratie, best seen by small boat in the dry season.","eat":"Fish amok: fish steamed in a coconut curry paste, often served in a banana-leaf cup.","fact":"Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s national flag."},
  "KI": {"name":"Kiribati","capital":"South Tarawa","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Atolls barely above the sea, WWII relics on Tarawa, and bonefishing on Kiritimati’s endless flats.","dontMiss":["Bonefishing the lagoon flats of Kiritimati","World War II remains along Betio on Tarawa","A visit to a village maneaba, the meeting house"],"lookFor":"Huge colonies of sooty terns and frigatebirds nesting on Kiritimati, also called Christmas Island.","eat":"Fresh reef fish, coconut, and babai, a giant swamp taro grown in pits dug into the coral.","fact":"Kiribati’s Line Islands are at UTC+14, so they’re the first place on Earth to start each new day."},
  "KM": {"name":"Comoros","capital":"Moroni","continent":"Africa","missing":"Volcanic islands that smell of ylang-ylang and cloves, an active volcano to climb, and almost no tourists.","dontMiss":["Climbing Mount Karthala, an active volcano","The old medina and harbour of Moroni","Mohéli Marine Park and its turtle beaches"],"lookFor":"Green turtles hauling up to nest on Itsamia beach, Mohéli, mostly at night.","eat":"Langouste à la vanille: lobster in a sauce made with the islands’ own vanilla.","fact":"The coelacanth, a fish once thought extinct for 66 million years, lives in deep water off the Comoros."},
  "KN": {"name":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","capital":"Basseterre","continent":"North America","missing":"Two small volcanic islands with a hilltop British fortress, rainforest on the slopes and monkeys living in it.","dontMiss":["Brimstone Hill Fortress","The St Kitts Scenic Railway around the island","Pinney’s Beach on Nevis, below Nevis Peak"],"lookFor":"Green vervet monkeys, brought from West Africa in the 1600s, in the hills and along the roadsides.","eat":"Stewed saltfish with spicy plantain, coconut dumplings and breadfruit, the national dish.","fact":"St Kitts was the first English colony in the Caribbean, settled in 1623."},
  "KP": {"name":"North Korea","capital":"Pyongyang","continent":"Asia","missing":"Open only to tightly guided tours, if at all: Mount Paektu’s crater lake, Goguryeo tomb murals and a closed world.","dontMiss":["Mount Paektu and its crater lake","The Goguryeo tombs near Pyongyang","Kaesong’s Koryo-era monuments"],"lookFor":"Kimilsungia orchids and Kimjongilia begonias, bred as national symbols, at Pyongyang flower shows.","eat":"Pyongyang naengmyeon: cold buckwheat noodles in a chilled broth.","fact":"Pyongyang’s 105-floor Ryugyong Hotel was begun in 1987 and has still never opened."},
  "KR": {"name":"South Korea","capital":"Seoul","continent":"Asia","missing":"Seoul’s food alleys and palaces, temple stays in the mountains, and fast trains that get you anywhere in hours.","dontMiss":["Gyeongbokgung Palace and the hanok lanes of Bukchon","A temple stay in the mountains","Jeju’s lava tubes and Hallasan"],"lookFor":"Red-crowned cranes wintering in the fields of Cheorwon, near the DMZ, from about November to February.","eat":"Korean barbecue grilled at the table, with kimchi and a spread of banchan.","fact":"Jeju’s haenyeo, women free-divers, harvest the seabed without air tanks; many are over 70."},
  "KW": {"name":"Kuwait","capital":"Kuwait City","continent":"Asia","missing":"A Gulf city with a souq that still feels local, dhow harbours, and an offshore island of Greek and Bronze Age ruins.","dontMiss":["The Kuwait Towers at dusk","Souq Al-Mubarakiya","Failaka Island’s Greek and Bronze Age ruins"],"lookFor":"Greater flamingos feeding on the mudflats at Jahra and Sulaibikhat Bay, in winter.","eat":"Mutabbaq samak: fried fish laid over rice cooked in its spiced broth.","fact":"Kuwait has no permanent rivers or lakes; most of its drinking water comes from desalination."},
  "KY": {"name":"Cayman Islands","capital":"George Town","continent":"North America","missing":"Reef walls that drop thousands of metres just offshore, and stingrays that gather on a sandbar to meet the boats.","dontMiss":["Stingray City sandbar","Seven Mile Beach","Diving Bloody Bay Wall off Little Cayman"],"lookFor":"Blue iguanas, found only on Grand Cayman, at the Queen Elizabeth II Botanic Park.","eat":"Fish rundown, fish simmered in coconut milk with breadfruit and plantain.","fact":"Grand Cayman has a place called Hell, with its own post office for postcards."},
  "KZ": {"name":"Kazakhstan","capital":"Astana","continent":"Asia","missing":"Steppe that seems to go on forever, mountain lakes outside Almaty, and Astana’s strange new skyline.","dontMiss":["Charyn Canyon, east of Almaty","The Kolsai lakes and the sunken forest of Kaindy","Big Almaty Lake in the Tian Shan"],"lookFor":"Wild apple trees, the ancestors of the apples we eat, in the foothill forests around Almaty.","eat":"Beshbarmak: boiled horse meat or mutton on wide noodles, with onion broth.","fact":"Yuri Gagarin’s 1961 flight, the first human spaceflight, launched from Baikonur in Kazakhstan."},
  "LA": {"name":"Laos","capital":"Vientiane","continent":"Asia","missing":"Monks collecting alms at dawn in Luang Prabang, slow boats on the Mekong, and karst valleys around Vang Vieng.","dontMiss":["The dawn alms procession in Luang Prabang","The two-day slow boat down the Mekong","Kuang Si Falls’ turquoise pools"],"lookFor":"Rescued Asiatic black bears at the sanctuary beside Kuang Si Falls.","eat":"Larb: minced meat or fish with lime, herbs and toasted rice, eaten with sticky rice.","fact":"Laos is the most heavily bombed country per person in history; the US dropped over 2 million tonnes in 1964–73."},
  "LB": {"name":"Lebanon","capital":"Beirut","continent":"Asia","missing":"Check the news before you go, but Roman temples, ancient cedars and a table of endless mezze are waiting.","dontMiss":["The temples of Baalbek","Byblos, one of the oldest continuously inhabited towns","Jeita Grotto’s caves by boat"],"lookFor":"Cedars of Lebanon, some over 1,000 years old, in the Cedars of God grove above the Qadisha Valley.","eat":"Mezze: hummus, parsley-heavy tabbouleh, kibbeh, and warm flatbread.","fact":"The word “Bible” traces back to Byblos, the Greek name for the Lebanese port that traded papyrus."},
  "LC": {"name":"Saint Lucia","capital":"Castries","continent":"North America","missing":"Two volcanic spires straight out of the sea, rainforest above black-sand bays, and a drive-in volcano near Soufrière.","dontMiss":["Hiking Gros Piton","The Sulphur Springs mud baths at Soufrière","Pigeon Island and its old British fort"],"lookFor":"The Saint Lucia parrot, found nowhere else, along the Des Cartiers rainforest trail.","eat":"Green fig and saltfish, the national dish: green bananas boiled and served with salt cod.","fact":"Saint Lucia, with under 200,000 people, has two Nobel laureates: Derek Walcott and Sir Arthur Lewis."},
  "LI": {"name":"Liechtenstein","capital":"Vaduz","continent":"Europe","missing":"A whole country you can walk across in a couple of days, with castle views and vineyards on Alpine slopes.","dontMiss":["The Liechtenstein Trail across the whole country","Vaduz Castle seen from the old town","The Kunstmuseum in Vaduz"],"lookFor":"Alpine marmots on the high pastures around Malbun in summer, whistling at hikers.","eat":"Käsknöpfle: little cheese dumplings with fried onions, with applesauce on the side.","fact":"Liechtenstein is one of only two doubly landlocked countries; the other is Uzbekistan."},
  "LK": {"name":"Sri Lanka","capital":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte","continent":"Asia","missing":"Ancient rock fortresses and Buddhist cities, tea hills you can ride a slow train through, and leopards in coastal parks.","dontMiss":["Climbing the rock fortress of Sigiriya","The train from Kandy to Ella through tea country","The old Dutch fort at Galle"],"lookFor":"Leopards in Yala National Park, which has one of the densest leopard populations in the world.","eat":"Rice and curry, many small dishes at once; and hoppers, bowl-shaped rice pancakes with an egg inside.","fact":"The Sri Maha Bodhi in Anuradhapura, planted in 288 BCE, is the oldest human-planted tree with a recorded date."},
  "LR": {"name":"Liberia","capital":"Monrovia","continent":"Africa","missing":"Surf at Robertsport, rainforest in Sapo National Park, and a history unlike any other country in Africa.","dontMiss":["Surfing at Robertsport","Sapo National Park’s rainforest","Providence Island in Monrovia, where the settlers landed"],"lookFor":"Former lab chimpanzees living on river islands near Marshall, fed by boat by their keepers.","eat":"Palm butter: a rich palm-nut stew with meat or fish, over rice.","fact":"Monrovia, the capital, is named after US President James Monroe."},
  "LS": {"name":"Lesotho","capital":"Maseru","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Kingdom in the Sky: pony trekking between Basotho villages, and mountain passes with the Drakensberg below.","dontMiss":["Driving up the Sani Pass","Pony trekking from Semonkong","Maletsunyane Falls, a 192 m single drop"],"lookFor":"The spiral aloe, found only in Lesotho’s high mountains, its leaves whorled in a perfect spiral.","eat":"Papa, a stiff maize porridge, with moroho greens and stewed meat.","fact":"Lesotho is the only country lying entirely above 1,000 m; its lowest point is about 1,400 m."},
  "LT": {"name":"Lithuania","capital":"Vilnius","continent":"Europe","missing":"Vilnius’s baroque old town, the dunes of the Curonian Spit, and a love of basketball that borders on religion.","dontMiss":["Vilnius Old Town and the Užupis district","The dunes of the Curonian Spit near Nida","Trakai Island Castle on its lake"],"lookFor":"White storks, the national bird, nesting on poles and barns in nearly every village in summer.","eat":"Cepelinai: big potato dumplings stuffed with meat, with sour cream and bacon.","fact":"Lithuania was the last country in Europe to adopt Christianity, officially in 1387."},
  "LU": {"name":"Luxembourg","capital":"Luxembourg City","continent":"Europe","missing":"A capital built on cliffs over river gorges, castles in the hills, and free buses and trains everywhere.","dontMiss":["The Bock Casemates under Luxembourg City","Vianden Castle","The sandstone gorges of the Mullerthal Trail"],"lookFor":"Vines on the Moselle slopes where Luxembourg’s crémant comes from, harvested in September and October.","eat":"Judd mat Gaardebounen: smoked pork collar with broad beans and potatoes.","fact":"Luxembourg is the world’s last remaining grand duchy."},
  "LV": {"name":"Latvia","capital":"Riga","continent":"Europe","missing":"Riga’s art nouveau streets, a long forested coast, and a midsummer festival that takes over the whole country.","dontMiss":["Riga’s art nouveau quarter around Alberta iela","Gauja National Park and the castles of Sigulda","Jāņi, the midsummer festival, in the countryside"],"lookFor":"Konik wild horses and herds of wild cattle grazing the meadows of Pape Nature Park.","eat":"Grey peas with bacon and onions, with dark rye bread.","fact":"About a third of central Riga is art nouveau, one of the highest concentrations of it anywhere."},
  "LY": {"name":"Libya","capital":"Tripoli","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Roman cities as complete as any anywhere, and Sahara rock art in the Acacus.","dontMiss":["Leptis Magna on the coast east of Tripoli","The Greek city of Cyrene","The old town of Ghadames"],"lookFor":"Date palms shading the gardens of Ghadames, an oasis town near the Algerian and Tunisian borders.","eat":"Bazeen: a dome of barley dough in a spicy lamb and tomato stew, eaten by hand.","fact":"Al-Aziziya, in Libya, held the world heat record of 58 °C for 90 years, until it was ruled invalid in 2012."},
  "MA": {"name":"Morocco","capital":"Rabat","continent":"Africa","missing":"Medinas with lanes too narrow for cars, mud-built villages in the mountains, and the Sahara a day’s drive past the Atlas.","dontMiss":["Getting lost in the Fez medina, and the Chouara tannery","A night in the dunes at Erg Chebbi","The blue lanes of Chefchaouen in the Rif"],"lookFor":"Barbary macaques in the cedar forests near Azrou, in the Middle Atlas.","eat":"Tagine slow-cooked under its clay cone, and on Fridays, couscous shared from one big plate.","fact":"Fez’s al-Qarawiyyin, founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, is often called the world’s oldest university still running."},
  "MC": {"name":"Monaco","capital":"Monaco","continent":"Europe","missing":"A whole country you can cross on foot in an hour, with a palace, a casino and a Grand Prix track that’s ordinary road the rest of the year.","dontMiss":["The Oceanographic Museum on its cliff above the sea","The changing of the guard at the Prince’s Palace, at 11:55","Walking the Grand Prix circuit, tunnel and hairpin included"],"lookFor":"Roses by the thousand in the Princess Grace Rose Garden in Fontvieille, at their best in May and June.","eat":"Barbagiuan, fried pastry parcels of chard and ricotta, Monaco’s national snack.","fact":"Monaco is the most densely populated country in the world: about 38,000 people on 2 square kilometres."},
  "MD": {"name":"Moldova","capital":"Chișinău","continent":"Europe","missing":"Wine country with almost no crowds, cellars that run for kilometres underground, and a cave monastery cut into a cliff above the Răut.","dontMiss":["The cave monastery at Orheiul Vechi","Cricova’s underground wine city","Soroca’s round fortress on the Dniester"],"lookFor":"White storks nesting on telegraph poles and chimneys in villages, from April to August.","eat":"Mămăligă, a firm cornmeal polenta, with brânză sheep’s cheese, sour cream and sarmale.","fact":"Mileștii Mici’s cellars, about 200 km of tunnels, hold the world’s largest wine collection, according to Guinness."},
  "ME": {"name":"Montenegro","capital":"Podgorica","continent":"Europe","missing":"A fjord-like bay with Venetian towns, a canyon over 1,000 m deep, and mountains right behind the beaches.","dontMiss":["Kotor’s old town and the climb up its walls","Rafting the Tara River canyon","Black Lake in Durmitor National Park"],"lookFor":"Dalmatian pelicans on Lake Skadar, the largest lake in the Balkans.","eat":"Njeguški pršut, smoked ham from the mountain village of Njeguši, with local cheese.","fact":"Montenegro uses the euro, though it isn’t in the eurozone or the EU."},
  "MG": {"name":"Madagascar","capital":"Antananarivo","continent":"Africa","missing":"Lemurs, baobabs and chameleons found nowhere else, on an island that split from India about 88 million years ago.","dontMiss":["The Avenue of the Baobabs at sunset","Tsingy de Bemaraha’s stone forest","Ranomafana’s rainforest and night walks"],"lookFor":"Indri, the largest lemur, whose wailing song carries through Andasibe’s forest in the morning.","eat":"Romazava: a beef and greens stew, with rice, which Malagasy eat at almost every meal.","fact":"Around 90 percent of Madagascar’s wildlife is found nowhere else."},
  "MH": {"name":"Marshall Islands","capital":"Majuro","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Ribbon-thin atolls a few feet above the sea, lagoons as clear as a swimming pool, and a way of life still built around canoes and fish.","dontMiss":["Laura beach at the far western end of Majuro","The navigation stick charts at the Alele Museum","Japanese WWII ruins on Taroa, in Maloelap Atoll"],"lookFor":"Reef sharks on almost any dive: in 2011 the country declared what was then the world’s largest shark sanctuary.","eat":"Breadfruit and pandanus, often baked in an earth oven, with reef fish cooked in coconut milk.","fact":"Bikini Atoll, where the US tested 23 nuclear weapons, gave its name to the swimsuit in 1946."},
  "MK": {"name":"North Macedonia","capital":"Skopje","continent":"Europe","missing":"A lake town with churches older than most European capitals, Byzantine frescoes in the mountains, and long lunches for very little.","dontMiss":["Ohrid’s old town and the church of St John at Kaneo","Matka Canyon by boat or kayak, outside Skopje","Bitola’s Širok Sokak and the ruins of Heraclea Lyncestis"],"lookFor":"Macedonian pine, or molika, a five-needled Balkan pine, on the slopes of Pelister National Park.","eat":"Tavče gravče, beans baked in a clay dish, and ajvar, the roasted red pepper relish made each autumn.","fact":"Lake Ohrid is over a million years old, one of the oldest lakes in the world."},
  "ML": {"name":"Mali","capital":"Bamako","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Djenné’s mud mosque, Dogon cliff villages, and some of the best music in Africa.","dontMiss":["Djenné’s Great Mosque and Monday market","The Dogon villages along the Bandiagara cliffs","Timbuktu’s mosques and manuscript libraries"],"lookFor":"Baobab trees around Dogon villages, their leaves dried and ground for sauce.","eat":"Tigadèguèna: meat in a peanut sauce, served with rice.","fact":"Mansa Musa, who ruled Mali in the 14th century, is often called the richest person who ever lived."},
  "MM": {"name":"Myanmar","capital":"Naypyidaw","continent":"Asia","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: thousands of temples on the Bagan plain, and a Buddhist culture few outsiders have seen up close.","dontMiss":["Sunrise over the temples of Bagan","Yangon’s Shwedagon Pagoda at dusk","Inle Lake’s stilt villages and leg-rowing fishermen"],"lookFor":"Irrawaddy dolphins in the Ayeyarwady north of Mandalay, where some still help fishermen drive fish into nets.","eat":"Mohinga, rice noodles in a fish and lemongrass broth, eaten for breakfast; and laphet thoke, pickled tea-leaf salad.","fact":"Myanmar moved its capital in 2005 to Naypyidaw, a new city built with 20-lane roads that see little traffic."},
  "MN": {"name":"Mongolia","capital":"Ulaanbaatar","continent":"Asia","missing":"Grassland to the horizon in every direction, nomad families who still move with the seasons, and night skies with almost no lights.","dontMiss":["A night with a herding family in their ger","The singing dunes of Khongoryn Els in the Gobi","Naadam in July: wrestling, archery and horse racing"],"lookFor":"Takhi, the wild Przewalski’s horses, coming down to graze at dusk in Hustai National Park.","eat":"Buuz, steamed mutton dumplings, and khuushuur, their fried cousins, eaten by the plateful at Naadam.","fact":"Mongolia is the most sparsely populated sovereign country, with about two people per square kilometre."},
  "MO": {"name":"Macau","capital":"Macau","continent":"Asia","missing":"A Portuguese old town of tiled squares and Baroque churches, and Macanese food you can’t get anywhere else.","dontMiss":["The Ruins of St Paul’s","Senado Square and the Historic Centre","Coloane village and its waterfront"],"lookFor":"Black-faced spoonbills wintering in the Cotai wetlands, roughly November to March.","eat":"Portuguese egg tarts, and minchi, Macanese minced meat with fried potato and soy.","fact":"Macau is the most densely populated territory in the world."},
  "MR": {"name":"Mauritania","capital":"Nouakchott","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Sahara at its emptiest: old caravan towns half lost to the dunes, and nights so quiet you can hear the sand move.","dontMiss":["The old manuscript libraries of Chinguetti","The Richat Structure, the “Eye of the Sahara”, near Ouadane","Banc d’Arguin, where the desert meets an Atlantic full of birds"],"lookFor":"Huge flocks of wintering shorebirds, with flamingos and pelicans, on the Banc d’Arguin from October to April.","eat":"Three rounds of mint tea, each sweeter than the last, with roast goat or camel and couscous.","fact":"Mauritania’s iron-ore trains run up to 2.5 km long, among the longest and heaviest trains in the world."},
  "MT": {"name":"Malta","capital":"Valletta","continent":"Europe","missing":"Seven thousand years of building on one small rock: temples older than the pyramids, a Baroque capital, and clear water off every cliff.","dontMiss":["St John’s Co-Cathedral in Valletta and its two Caravaggios","The Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum, booked weeks ahead","A ferry to Gozo for the Ġgantija temples and the Dwejra coast"],"lookFor":"The blue rock thrush, Malta’s national bird, singing from the sea cliffs at Dingli and the bastions of Valletta.","eat":"Stuffat tal-fenek, rabbit slow-cooked in wine and garlic, and pastizzi, flaky ricotta or pea pastries, for breakfast.","fact":"Malta was awarded the George Cross as a whole nation in 1942 for holding out under siege, and it’s still on the flag."},
  "MU": {"name":"Mauritius","capital":"Port Louis","continent":"Africa","missing":"A volcanic island ringed by reef, where Hindu temples, Creole music and Chinese shops share the same small towns.","dontMiss":["Le Morne Brabant and the story of the escaped slaves who hid there","The viewpoints of Black River Gorges National Park","The giant water lilies at the Pamplemousses botanical garden"],"lookFor":"Pink pigeons and Aldabra giant tortoises on Île aux Aigrettes, a restored islet off the southeast coast.","eat":"Dholl puri, thin flatbreads filled with split peas, rolled round bean curry and chutney at a street stall.","fact":"The dodo lived only on Mauritius and was gone within a century of Dutch sailors landing in 1598."},
  "MV": {"name":"Maldives","capital":"Malé","continent":"Asia","missing":"Reefs that start at the beach, and since guesthouses were allowed on local islands, it needn’t cost a fortune.","dontMiss":["Snorkeling a house reef straight from the beach","Manta rays at Hanifaru Bay, in Baa Atoll","A stay on a local island like Maafushi or Thoddoo"],"lookFor":"Whale sharks off South Ari Atoll, where they can be seen all year.","eat":"Garudhiya: a clear tuna broth eaten with rice, lime, chilli and onion.","fact":"The Maldives is the world’s lowest country, with an average height of about 1.5 m above sea level."},
  "MW": {"name":"Malawi","capital":"Lilongwe","continent":"Africa","missing":"Lake Malawi’s clear water and its hundreds of fish species, easygoing lakeside villages, and quiet safaris.","dontMiss":["Snorkeling at Cape Maclear","Liwonde National Park by boat on the Shire River","The high grasslands of the Nyika Plateau"],"lookFor":"African fish eagles calling over Lake Malawi, and hippos in the Shire River at Liwonde.","eat":"Chambo, a tilapia from the lake, grilled and served with nsima.","fact":"Lake Malawi has more fish species than any other lake, perhaps 700 to 1,000, most of them cichlids."},
  "MX": {"name":"Mexico","capital":"Mexico City","continent":"North America","missing":"Food that changes every few hundred kilometres, pyramids you can walk among, and cities that live their best life in the street.","dontMiss":["Teotihuacan’s Avenue of the Dead early, before the heat","Oaxaca’s markets and the mezcal villages around it","A swim in a Yucatán cenote"],"lookFor":"Monarch butterflies covering the fir trees of the Michoacán reserves, from about November to March.","eat":"Tacos al pastor, spit-roasted pork with pineapple, and in Oaxaca, mole made with dozens of ingredients.","fact":"Mexico City is sinking, in places by up to half a metre a year, as water is pumped from the old lake bed beneath it."},
  "MY": {"name":"Malaysia","capital":"Kuala Lumpur","continent":"Asia","missing":"Hawker food from three cuisines, Borneo’s rainforests and orangutans, and islands with clear water.","dontMiss":["Eating through George Town’s hawker stalls in Penang","Sepilok and the Kinabatangan River on Borneo","Diving at Sipadan"],"lookFor":"Rafflesia, the world’s largest flower, which blooms for only a few days in Sabah and Sarawak forests.","eat":"Nasi lemak: coconut rice with sambal, anchovies, peanuts and egg, often for breakfast.","fact":"Malaysia’s king is chosen every five years from among nine hereditary state rulers."},
  "MZ": {"name":"Mozambique","capital":"Maputo","continent":"Africa","missing":"Some 2,500 km of coast: empty beaches, coral islands and old Swahili-Portuguese towns, with piri-piri on everything.","dontMiss":["The old stone town on Ilha de Moçambique","Sailing a dhow around the Bazaruto Archipelago","The Quirimbas islands off Pemba"],"lookFor":"Whale sharks and manta rays off Tofo, and humpback whales passing from July to October.","eat":"Prawns grilled with piri-piri, and matapa, cassava leaves cooked with ground peanut and coconut.","fact":"Mozambique’s flag has an AK-47 on it, the only national flag with a modern rifle."},
  "NA": {"name":"Namibia","capital":"Windhoek","continent":"Africa","missing":"Red dunes running into the Atlantic, desert-adapted elephants, and the kind of empty road where you pass three cars all day.","dontMiss":["Sunrise from the dunes at Sossusvlei and Deadvlei","Etosha’s waterholes in the dry season","Kolmanskop, the diamond town filling up with sand"],"lookFor":"Welwitschia, a desert plant that can live for over a thousand years, along the Welwitschia Drive near Swakopmund.","eat":"Kapana, beef grilled at street stalls in Windhoek’s Katutura and dipped in chilli salt.","fact":"The Hoba meteorite, near Grootfontein, is the largest known meteorite on Earth, at around 60 tonnes."},
  "NC": {"name":"New Caledonia","capital":"Nouméa","continent":"Oceania","missing":"One of the world’s largest lagoons, Kanak culture, and red-earth mountains with forests found nowhere else.","dontMiss":["The Tjibaou Cultural Centre in Nouméa","The Isle of Pines and its natural pool","Diving or kayaking the lagoon"],"lookFor":"The kagu, a grey flightless bird found only here, in the Parc de la Rivière Bleue.","eat":"Bougna, yam, sweet potato and chicken in coconut milk, wrapped in banana leaves and cooked on hot stones.","fact":"New Caledonia is home to Amborella, a shrub thought to be the oldest surviving line of flowering plants."},
  "NE": {"name":"Niger","capital":"Niamey","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Agadez’s mud-brick mosque, the Aïr mountains, and Tuareg camel caravans crossing the Ténéré.","dontMiss":["The mud minaret of the Grand Mosque in Agadez","The Aïr Mountains and the edge of the Ténéré desert","The Niger River at Niamey by pirogue"],"lookFor":"West Africa’s last wild giraffes, roaming the farmland around Kouré, east of Niamey.","eat":"Kilishi, thin sheets of spiced dried meat, and millet paste with okra or baobab-leaf sauce.","fact":"The Ténéré Tree, once the most isolated tree on Earth, was knocked down by a truck in 1973."},
  "NG": {"name":"Nigeria","capital":"Abuja","continent":"Africa","missing":"Africa’s biggest music and film industries at home, Lagos at full volume, and some of the best street food on the continent.","dontMiss":["The Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove and its sculptures","The canopy walkway at the Lekki Conservation Centre in Lagos","The view from Olumo Rock in Abeokuta"],"lookFor":"Drills, rare forest primates related to baboons, at the rescue sanctuary in Calabar.","eat":"Jollof rice, smoky from the pot, and suya, spiced beef skewers grilled at night by the roadside.","fact":"Nollywood makes more films a year than Hollywood; by UNESCO’s count only India’s industry makes more."},
  "NI": {"name":"Nicaragua","capital":"Managua","continent":"North America","missing":"Lakes with volcanoes rising out of them, colonial towns painted in bright colours, and Pacific surf with few people on it.","dontMiss":["Ometepe, the twin-volcano island in Lake Nicaragua","Granada’s colonial streets and the lake isletas","León’s cathedral roof, and volcano boarding on Cerro Negro"],"lookFor":"Olive ridley turtles nesting by the thousand at La Flor and Chacocente, roughly July to January.","eat":"Gallo pinto, rice and red beans fried together, with fried plantain and fresh cheese at breakfast.","fact":"Lake Nicaragua has bull sharks, which swim up the San Juan River from the Caribbean."},
  "NL": {"name":"Netherlands","capital":"Amsterdam","continent":"Europe","missing":"Cities built for bikes rather than cars, Golden Age paintings in the towns where they were made, and a country engineered out of the sea.","dontMiss":["The Night Watch and the Vermeers at the Rijksmuseum","The 19 windmills along the dikes at Kinderdijk","Utrecht or Delft by canal, without Amsterdam’s crowds"],"lookFor":"Tulip fields around Lisse and the Bollenstreek, at their peak from mid to late April.","eat":"Raw herring with chopped onion from a street stand, held by the tail, best when the new catch arrives in June.","fact":"About a quarter of the Netherlands lies below sea level, kept dry by dikes, dunes and pumps."},
  "NO": {"name":"Norway","capital":"Oslo","continent":"Europe","missing":"Fjords so deep and steep the ferries look small, summer nights with no dark at all, and northern lights in winter.","dontMiss":["Geirangerfjord or Nærøyfjord by boat","The Lofoten Islands and their red fishing cabins","The Flåm Railway, or the Bergen line over the mountains"],"lookFor":"Orcas and humpbacks feeding on herring in the fjords near Tromsø and Skjervøy, November to January.","eat":"Brunost, sweet brown whey cheese, on bread or waffles; and skrei, fresh winter cod, in Lofoten.","fact":"Norway’s Lærdal Tunnel, 24.5 km long, is the longest road tunnel in the world."},
  "NP": {"name":"Nepal","capital":"Kathmandu","continent":"Asia","missing":"Eight of the world’s ten highest mountains, and trails between them where tea houses feed you dal bhat for weeks.","dontMiss":["Bhaktapur and the Durbar Squares of the Kathmandu Valley","A tea-house trek to Everest Base Camp or round Annapurna","Chitwan’s jungle by jeep or dugout canoe"],"lookFor":"Greater one-horned rhinos grazing the grasslands of Chitwan, best seen from October to March.","eat":"Dal bhat, lentils with rice and vegetables, refilled until you stop; and momos, steamed dumplings.","fact":"Nepal’s flag, two stacked pennants, is the only national flag that isn’t a rectangle."},
  "NR": {"name":"Nauru","capital":"Yaren","continent":"Oceania","missing":"A whole country you can drive around in under half an hour, with a coral-pinnacle coast and a mined-out interior like another planet.","dontMiss":["Anibare Bay, the island’s best beach","Command Ridge and its WWII Japanese guns","Buada Lagoon, ringed by palms"],"lookFor":"Frigatebirds riding the wind off the coast, birds Nauruans traditionally caught and tamed.","eat":"Fresh tuna and reef fish cooked with coconut, with breadfruit and pandanus.","fact":"Nauru is the world’s smallest republic and has no official capital; the government works from Yaren."},
  "NZ": {"name":"New Zealand","capital":"Wellington","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Fjords, volcanoes, glaciers and surf beaches within a day’s drive of each other, and long trails with huts the whole way.","dontMiss":["Milford Sound, by boat or on the Milford Track","The Tongariro Alpine Crossing past the volcanic lakes","Rotorua’s geothermal valleys and Māori culture"],"lookFor":"Kea, the cheeky alpine parrot, at Arthur’s Pass and on the road to Milford Sound.","eat":"A hāngī, meat and vegetables cooked in an earth oven; and fish and chips eaten on the beach.","fact":"In 1893, New Zealand became the first self-governing country to give women the vote."},
  "OM": {"name":"Oman","capital":"Muscat","continent":"Asia","missing":"Arabia without the skyscrapers: mud-brick forts, mountain villages above green wadis, and dunes that run down to the coast.","dontMiss":["Swimming in the pools of Wadi Shab","Nizwa’s fort and its Friday livestock market","A night in the dunes of the Wahiba Sands"],"lookFor":"Green turtles coming ashore at night to nest at Ras al Jinz, all year round.","eat":"Shuwa, lamb rubbed with spices and slow-cooked underground for a day or more, made for Eid.","fact":"Each summer the khareef monsoon turns the hills around Salalah green while the rest of Arabia bakes."},
  "PA": {"name":"Panama","capital":"Panama City","continent":"North America","missing":"A canal where you can watch ships climb through locks, rainforest half an hour from the skyline, and two oceans an hour apart.","dontMiss":["Watching ships pass the Miraflores Locks","Casco Viejo, Panama City’s old quarter","The Guna Yala (San Blas) islands"],"lookFor":"Sloths and toucans along Pipeline Road in Soberanía National Park.","eat":"Sancocho, chicken soup with yam and culantro, the national dish.","fact":"Because the isthmus curves, the canal’s Pacific end lies east of its Atlantic end."},
  "PE": {"name":"Peru","capital":"Lima","continent":"South America","missing":"Inca stonework in high Andean valleys, Amazon rainforest on the same trip, and Lima, one of the best cities in the world to eat in.","dontMiss":["Machu Picchu, ideally walked into on the Inca Trail","Cusco and the terraces and markets of the Sacred Valley","Lake Titicaca and its floating reed islands"],"lookFor":"Andean condors riding the morning thermals over the Colca Canyon at Cruz del Cóndor.","eat":"Ceviche, raw fish cured in lime with chilli and red onion, eaten at lunch when the fish is fresh.","fact":"The potato was domesticated in the Andes thousands of years ago, and Peru still grows thousands of varieties."},
  "PF": {"name":"French Polynesia","capital":"Papeete","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Lagoons in every shade of blue, overwater bungalows on Bora Bora, and old Polynesian sites in the Marquesas.","dontMiss":["Bora Bora’s lagoon by outrigger canoe","Cook’s Bay and Opunohu Bay on Moorea","Rangiroa’s Tiputa Pass, for dolphins and sharks"],"lookFor":"Humpback whales off Moorea and Rurutu, roughly August to October.","eat":"Poisson cru, raw tuna in lime juice and coconut milk.","fact":"French Polynesia’s 118 islands are spread over an area of ocean about the size of Western Europe."},
  "PG": {"name":"Papua New Guinea","capital":"Port Moresby","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Highland festivals with feathered dancers, river villages reached only by canoe, and reefs and WWII wrecks few divers see.","dontMiss":["The Goroka or Mount Hagen highland shows","Spirit houses along the Sepik River, by canoe","Walking the Kokoda Track"],"lookFor":"The kumul, the plumed bird on the national flag, displaying at dawn in the forests of the Tari valley.","eat":"Mumu, pork, sweet potato and greens cooked on hot stones in a pit, made for feasts.","fact":"Papua New Guinea has over 800 living languages, more than any other country."},
  "PH": {"name":"Philippines","capital":"Manila","continent":"Asia","missing":"Over 7,000 islands of limestone lagoons and coral reefs, mountain rice terraces, and a fiesta in some town almost every week.","dontMiss":["Island-hopping the lagoons of El Nido, Palawan","The rice terraces of Banaue and Batad","Moalboal’s sardine run, a short swim off Cebu"],"lookFor":"The Philippine tarsier, palm-sized with enormous eyes, at the tarsier sanctuary in Corella, Bohol.","eat":"Adobo, meat braised in vinegar, soy and garlic; and lechón, whole roast pig, at every fiesta.","fact":"Jeepneys, the painted shared buses, began as surplus US Army jeeps left behind after World War II."},
  "PK": {"name":"Pakistan","capital":"Islamabad","continent":"Asia","missing":"Five of the world’s 14 peaks over 8,000 m, Mughal Lahore, and hospitality so insistent you’ll struggle to pay for a cup of tea.","dontMiss":["The Karakoram Highway up to the Hunza Valley","Lahore’s Badshahi Mosque and the walled city","Fairy Meadows, below Nanga Parbat"],"lookFor":"Markhor, the spiral-horned wild goat and national animal, on the cliffs of Chitral Gol in winter.","eat":"Nihari, beef stew slow-cooked overnight and eaten for breakfast in Lahore and Karachi.","fact":"The Karakoram Highway tops out at 4,693 m at the Khunjerab Pass, the highest paved border crossing in the world."},
  "PL": {"name":"Poland","capital":"Warsaw","continent":"Europe","missing":"Old towns rebuilt brick by brick, a salt mine with chapels carved underground, and forests with Europe’s last wild bison.","dontMiss":["Kraków’s main square and Wawel Castle","The underground chapels of the Wieliczka Salt Mine","The Auschwitz-Birkenau memorial, with a guide"],"lookFor":"European bison in Białowieża Forest, one of the last stretches of primeval woodland in Europe.","eat":"Pierogi stuffed with potato and cheese, meat or berries; and żurek, a sour rye soup.","fact":"After WWII, Warsaw’s old town was rebuilt using 18th-century paintings by Bernardo Bellotto as a guide."},
  "PR": {"name":"Puerto Rico","capital":"San Juan","continent":"North America","missing":"Old San Juan’s blue cobbles and forts, a rainforest an hour from the city, and bays that glow at night.","dontMiss":["El Morro and the city walls of Old San Juan","El Yunque rainforest","Kayaking Mosquito Bay on Vieques after dark"],"lookFor":"Coquí frogs, tiny and loud, calling “ko-kee” all night across the island.","eat":"Mofongo, mashed fried plantain with garlic and pork crackling, often filled with shrimp.","fact":"Puerto Ricans are US citizens but can’t vote for president unless they live in a state."},
  "PS": {"name":"Palestine","capital":"Ramallah (seat of government)","continent":"Asia","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Bethlehem and Hebron’s old cities, Jericho’s ancient oasis, and markets full of olives and za’atar.","dontMiss":["The Church of the Nativity in Bethlehem","Jericho and the mosaics of Hisham’s Palace","The old city souq in Hebron"],"lookFor":"Olive trees, some centuries old, across the West Bank hills, with the harvest in October.","eat":"Musakhan, roast chicken on taboon bread with slow-cooked onions and sumac.","fact":"Jericho, about 250 m below sea level, is the lowest city in the world."},
  "PT": {"name":"Portugal","capital":"Lisbon","continent":"Europe","missing":"Tiled cities on steep hills, Atlantic beaches with cliffs behind them, and wine and seafood that cost less than you’d expect.","dontMiss":["Lisbon’s Alfama, and a night of fado","Porto’s riverside and the port lodges across the bridge","The Algarve’s cliffs and the sea cave at Benagil"],"lookFor":"White storks nesting on sea stacks on the Costa Vicentina, the only place they’re known to nest on sea cliffs.","eat":"Pastéis de nata, custard tarts with blistered tops, warm with cinnamon; and grilled sardines in June.","fact":"The Anglo-Portuguese alliance, signed in 1373, is the oldest alliance in the world still in force."},
  "PW": {"name":"Palau","capital":"Ngerulmud","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Hundreds of jungle-topped limestone islands in a clear lagoon, and dives where sharks and mantas are the usual.","dontMiss":["Kayaking among the Rock Islands","Diving or snorkelling the reef wall at Blue Corner","The Milky Way, a lagoon floored with white limestone mud"],"lookFor":"Manta rays at the cleaning stations in German Channel, most of the year.","eat":"Taro, reef fish and coconut crab; braver eaters try fruit bat soup, a Palauan specialty.","fact":"Palau has visitors sign a pledge to protect its environment, stamped into their passport on arrival."},
  "PY": {"name":"Paraguay","capital":"Asunción","continent":"South America","missing":"Jesuit mission ruins in the red earth, the Chaco’s dry forest full of wildlife, and a country where most people speak Guaraní.","dontMiss":["The Jesuit mission ruins at Trinidad and Jesús","The Itaipú Dam on the Paraná","The Chaco by road, for wildlife and Mennonite towns"],"lookFor":"Jabiru storks and caimans in the wetlands of the Chaco, easiest to see in the dry season.","eat":"Sopa paraguaya, a “soup” that’s really a solid cornbread with cheese and onion; and chipa rings.","fact":"Paraguay’s flag has a different emblem on each side: the national seal on the front, the treasury seal on the back."},
  "QA": {"name":"Qatar","capital":"Doha","continent":"Asia","missing":"A desert peninsula where dunes meet a warm sea, a museum shaped like a desert rose, and old souqs beside new towers.","dontMiss":["Souq Waqif in the evening","The National Museum and the Museum of Islamic Art","The Inland Sea at Khor Al Adaid, by 4x4"],"lookFor":"Flamingos wading among the grey mangroves at Al Thakhira, north of Doha, mostly in winter.","eat":"Machboos, spiced rice with chicken or lamb, dried lime and a little rosewater.","fact":"Qatar’s flag is the only national flag more than twice as wide as it is tall, at a ratio of 11:28."},
  "RO": {"name":"Romania","capital":"Bucharest","continent":"Europe","missing":"Medieval Saxon towns, painted monasteries, villages where horse carts still share the road, and the deep forests of the Carpathians.","dontMiss":["Sighișoara and Brașov in Transylvania","The painted monasteries of Bucovina","The Danube Delta by small boat"],"lookFor":"Brown bears from a hide near Brașov; the Carpathians hold Europe’s largest population outside Russia.","eat":"Sarmale, cabbage rolls stuffed with pork and rice, with mămăligă and sour cream.","fact":"Bucharest’s Palace of the Parliament is often cited as the heaviest building in the world."},
  "RS": {"name":"Serbia","capital":"Belgrade","continent":"Europe","missing":"Belgrade’s late nights where two rivers meet, medieval monasteries in the hills, and the Danube’s gorge at the Iron Gates.","dontMiss":["Kalemegdan fortress above the Sava and the Danube","Studenica Monastery","The Đerdap gorge on the Danube"],"lookFor":"Long-eared owls, hundreds at a time, roosting in the trees of Kikinda’s main square in winter.","eat":"Ćevapi and pljeskavica from the grill, with kajmak and raw onion.","fact":"Nikola Tesla’s ashes are kept in a gilded sphere at the Nikola Tesla Museum in Belgrade."},
  "RU": {"name":"Russia","capital":"Moscow","continent":"Europe","missing":"When travel opens up again: the Hermitage, onion-domed old towns, and the Trans-Siberian, 9,289 km of forest and steppe.","dontMiss":["St Petersburg’s Hermitage, and the canals during the white nights","Red Square and the Kremlin","Lake Baikal, by the Trans-Siberian"],"lookFor":"Nerpa, the only seal that lives purely in fresh water, on the rocks of Baikal’s Ushkany Islands in early summer.","eat":"Pelmeni, small meat dumplings with sour cream, and blini with smoked fish or caviar.","fact":"Russia and the US are under 4 km apart, at the Diomede Islands in the Bering Strait."},
  "RW": {"name":"Rwanda","capital":"Kigali","continent":"Africa","missing":"Mountain gorillas in volcano forests, green hills farmed to the top, and a capital that’s remarkably clean and calm.","dontMiss":["Gorilla trekking in Volcanoes National Park","The Kigali Genocide Memorial","The shore of Lake Kivu at Gisenyi or Kibuye"],"lookFor":"Golden monkeys in the bamboo forest of Volcanoes National Park.","eat":"Goat brochettes grilled over charcoal, with fried plantain and akabanga, Rwanda’s fierce chilli oil.","fact":"Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and bags can be taken from visitors’ luggage on arrival."},
  "SA": {"name":"Saudi Arabia","capital":"Riyadh","continent":"Asia","missing":"Nabataean tombs carved in desert sandstone at AlUla, Red Sea reefs few divers have seen, and old mud-brick towns like Diriyah.","dontMiss":["The tombs of Hegra at AlUla","Al-Balad, the old coral-stone town of Jeddah","The Edge of the World cliffs northwest of Riyadh"],"lookFor":"Herds of dromedaries along the desert highways; Riyadh’s camel market shows them up close.","eat":"Kabsa, spiced rice with lamb or chicken shared from a big platter, with dates and cardamom coffee.","fact":"Saudi Arabia has no permanent rivers, only wadis that run after rain."},
  "SB": {"name":"Solomon Islands","capital":"Honiara","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Lagoons full of WWII wrecks, villages built on artificial islands, and reefs that see very few divers.","dontMiss":["Marovo Lagoon and its carving villages","Diving the WWII wrecks of Iron Bottom Sound","The artificial islands of Langa Langa Lagoon"],"lookFor":"Leatherback and hawksbill turtles nesting on Tetepare, a protected and uninhabited island.","eat":"Fresh fish and slippery cabbage cooked in coconut milk, with taro or cassava.","fact":"When JFK’s PT-109 sank here in 1943, islanders carried his message, scratched on a coconut, to get help."},
  "SC": {"name":"Seychelles","capital":"Victoria","continent":"Africa","missing":"Granite boulders on white beaches, giant tortoises, and the coco de mer palm, found wild on only two islands.","dontMiss":["Anse Source d’Argent on La Digue","The coco de mer forest of Vallée de Mai on Praslin","The trails of Morne Seychellois National Park on Mahé"],"lookFor":"Aldabra giant tortoises roaming free on Curieuse Island, a short boat ride from Praslin.","eat":"Octopus curry in coconut milk, and grilled fish with Creole chilli sauce.","fact":"The coco de mer produces the largest seed of any plant, weighing up to 25 kg."},
  "SD": {"name":"Sudan","capital":"Khartoum","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: more pyramids than Egypt at Meroë, the meeting of the two Niles, and people known for their hospitality.","dontMiss":["The pyramids of Meroë in the desert","Jebel Barkal, the sacred mountain at Karima","The meeting of the Blue and White Nile at Khartoum"],"lookFor":"Doum palms along the Nile in the north; their fruit tastes a little like gingerbread.","eat":"Ful medames, slow-cooked fava beans with oil, cumin and cheese, eaten for breakfast.","fact":"Sudan was Africa’s largest country until South Sudan became independent in 2011."},
  "SE": {"name":"Sweden","capital":"Stockholm","continent":"Europe","missing":"A capital spread over 14 islands, the right to walk and camp almost anywhere, and midnight sun in Lapland.","dontMiss":["Gamla Stan, then a ferry into the Stockholm archipelago","The 17th-century warship at the Vasa Museum","Abisko, for the northern lights"],"lookFor":"Moose grazing at the edge of the forest at dusk; Sweden has one of the densest moose populations anywhere.","eat":"Kanelbullar, cinnamon buns, with coffee at fika; and herring pickled a dozen ways at midsummer.","fact":"Sweden has about 267,000 islands, more than any other country."},
  "SG": {"name":"Singapore","capital":"Singapore","continent":"Asia","missing":"Hawker centres where a great meal costs a few dollars, rainforest inside the city, and Chinese, Malay and Indian food on one street.","dontMiss":["Eating your way through Maxwell or Lau Pa Sat hawker centres","The Supertrees at Gardens by the Bay at night","The TreeTop Walk at MacRitchie Reservoir"],"lookFor":"Smooth-coated otters along the Singapore River and in Gardens by the Bay, often early in the morning.","eat":"Hainanese chicken rice: poached chicken, rice cooked in chicken stock, and chilli sauce.","fact":"Singapore’s land area has grown by about a quarter since independence, through land reclamation."},
  "SI": {"name":"Slovenia","capital":"Ljubljana","continent":"Europe","missing":"Alps, caves and the Adriatic within two hours’ drive, and a small capital where the river is the main street.","dontMiss":["Lake Bled, and Lake Bohinj next door","The underground canyon of the Škocjan Caves","The emerald river of the Soča Valley"],"lookFor":"The olm, a blind pale cave salamander, in the tanks at Postojna Cave.","eat":"Kranjska klobasa, the Carniolan sausage, and potica, a rolled nut cake for holidays.","fact":"Slovenia is about 58% forest, one of the most forested countries in Europe."},
  "SK": {"name":"Slovakia","capital":"Bratislava","continent":"Europe","missing":"The granite peaks of the High Tatras, a castle on half the hilltops, and wooden churches in the villages of the east.","dontMiss":["Spiš Castle, one of the largest in Central Europe","Hiking in the High Tatras from Štrbské Pleso","Banská Štiavnica, an old silver-mining town"],"lookFor":"Tatra chamois on the high ridges of the Tatras, easiest to spot in summer.","eat":"Bryndzové halušky, small potato dumplings with sheep’s cheese and bacon.","fact":"Slovakia makes more cars per person than any other country in the world."},
  "SL": {"name":"Sierra Leone","capital":"Freetown","continent":"Africa","missing":"Beaches backed by forested mountains just outside the capital, islands with old slave-trade forts, and rare monkeys in the rainforest.","dontMiss":["The Freetown Peninsula beaches, River No. 2 and Bureh","The slave-trade fort ruins on Bunce Island","The rainforest of Tiwai Island"],"lookFor":"Some of Tiwai Island’s eleven primate species, including Diana monkeys and red colobus.","eat":"Cassava leaf stew, cooked with palm oil and fish or meat, over rice.","fact":"Freetown was founded in 1792 by formerly enslaved Black Loyalists who sailed from Nova Scotia."},
  "SM": {"name":"San Marino","capital":"City of San Marino","continent":"Europe","missing":"A hilltop republic older than most countries, with three towers on a cliff and views across Italy to the Adriatic.","dontMiss":["Walking between the Three Towers on Monte Titano","The Basilica and the Palazzo Pubblico","The cable car down to Borgo Maggiore"],"lookFor":"Swifts screaming around the towers of Monte Titano on summer evenings.","eat":"Torta Tre Monti, a layered wafer cake shaped after the three towers.","fact":"San Marino claims to be the world’s oldest surviving republic, founded in 301."},
  "SN": {"name":"Senegal","capital":"Dakar","continent":"Africa","missing":"Dakar’s music and art scene, mangrove creeks in the Saloum delta, and teranga, the habit of treating strangers as guests.","dontMiss":["Gorée Island and the House of Slaves","Saint-Louis, the old colonial island town","The Sine-Saloum delta by pirogue"],"lookFor":"Pelicans and flamingos in their thousands at Djoudj bird sanctuary, from November to April.","eat":"Thiéboudienne, fish and rice cooked in tomato with vegetables, the national dish.","fact":"Dakar’s African Renaissance Monument, at 49 m, is the tallest statue in Africa."},
  "SO": {"name":"Somalia","capital":"Mogadishu","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: mainland Africa’s longest coastline, old coral-stone towns, and Neolithic rock art at Laas Geel.","dontMiss":["The painted caves of Laas Geel, near Hargeisa","Lido Beach in Mogadishu","Hamar Weyne, Mogadishu’s old quarter"],"lookFor":"Frankincense trees on the dry limestone hills of the north, tapped for their resin.","eat":"Bariis iskukaris, spiced rice with goat or camel, eaten with a banana on the side.","fact":"Laas Geel’s cave paintings of cattle, thousands of years old, were first studied by archaeologists in 2002."},
  "SR": {"name":"Suriname","capital":"Paramaribo","continent":"South America","missing":"Rainforest over more than 90% of the country, a wooden Dutch colonial capital, and Javanese, Hindustani and Maroon food in one city.","dontMiss":["Paramaribo’s wooden old town and Fort Zeelandia","A river trip to the Maroon villages of the upper Suriname","Brownsberg Nature Park, above the Brokopondo reservoir"],"lookFor":"Leatherback turtles nesting at Galibi and Matapica, roughly April to August.","eat":"Pom, a baked dish of chicken and grated pomtajer root, served on bread at every party.","fact":"Suriname is the only country in South America where Dutch is the official language."},
  "SS": {"name":"South Sudan","capital":"Juba","continent":"Africa","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: one of Africa’s largest animal migrations, the Sudd swamp on the Nile, and Mundari cattle camps.","dontMiss":["The Sudd wetlands on the White Nile","A Mundari cattle camp near Terekeka","Boma and Badingilo national parks"],"lookFor":"White-eared kob moving through Boma and Badingilo in herds of thousands on their seasonal migration.","eat":"Kisra, a thin sorghum flatbread, with okra, peanut or fish stews.","fact":"South Sudan became the world’s newest country on 9 July 2011."},
  "ST": {"name":"São Tomé and Príncipe","capital":"São Tomé","continent":"Africa","missing":"Two volcanic islands on the equator with old cocoa plantations, rainforest down to the sea, and almost no other tourists.","dontMiss":["Pico Cão Grande, the volcanic needle in the south","An old cocoa roça such as Agostinho Neto or São João","Praia Banana on Príncipe"],"lookFor":"Humpback whales offshore from July to October, and sea turtles nesting on the beaches from November to March.","eat":"Calulu, fish or chicken stewed with vegetables and leaves, with banana and breadfruit.","fact":"The equator runs across Ilhéu das Rolas, a tiny islet off São Tomé, where a monument marks the line."},
  "SV": {"name":"El Salvador","capital":"San Salvador","continent":"North America","missing":"Volcano hikes, Pacific surf breaks and a Maya village buried by ash, all within a couple of hours of each other.","dontMiss":["Joya de Cerén, the “Pompeii of the Americas”","Santa Ana volcano and its green crater lake","El Tunco’s surf beach at sunset"],"lookFor":"The torogoz, or turquoise-browed motmot, the national bird, swinging its long tail like a pendulum.","eat":"Pupusas: thick corn tortillas stuffed with cheese, beans or pork, with curtido slaw.","fact":"El Salvador is the only Central American country without a Caribbean coastline."},
  "SY": {"name":"Syria","capital":"Damascus","continent":"Asia","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Damascus, one of the oldest lived-in cities on Earth, Aleppo’s souqs, and Crusader castles in the hills.","dontMiss":["The Umayyad Mosque and the old city of Damascus","Krak des Chevaliers","The ruins of Palmyra"],"lookFor":"Damask roses in the fields of al-Mrah, north of Damascus, picked at dawn in May.","eat":"Kibbeh, spiced lamb in a cracked-wheat shell, and Aleppo’s kebab with sour cherries.","fact":"One of the oldest known pieces of written music was found at Ugarit, on a clay tablet about 3,400 years old."},
  "SZ": {"name":"Eswatini","capital":"Mbabane","continent":"Africa","missing":"A small mountain kingdom with rhino reserves, the Umhlanga reed dance, and craft markets in green valleys.","dontMiss":["The Umhlanga reed dance, in late August or September","Mlilwane Wildlife Sanctuary, by bike or on horseback","The craft markets of the Ezulwini Valley"],"lookFor":"White rhinos at the waterholes of Hlane Royal National Park.","eat":"Sishwala, a stiff maize porridge, with stewed meat or beans.","fact":"Eswatini is one of the last absolute monarchies in the world; King Mswati III has reigned since 1986."},
  "TD": {"name":"Chad","capital":"N’Djamena","continent":"Africa","missing":"Not an easy trip, but it has the Ennedi’s sandstone arches and rock art, and Zakouma’s growing elephant herds.","dontMiss":["The arches and rock art of the Ennedi Plateau","Zakouma National Park in the dry season","The Guelta d’Archei, where camel herds come to drink"],"lookFor":"Kordofan giraffes and elephant herds at Zakouma National Park, from about December to May.","eat":"Boule: a ball of millet or sorghum dough with an okra or dried-fish sauce.","fact":"Toumaï, a skull about 7 million years old found in Chad’s Djurab Desert in 2001, is among the oldest known hominins."},
  "TG": {"name":"Togo","capital":"Lomé","continent":"Africa","missing":"A narrow country you can cross from beach to savannah in a day, Tamberma mud tower-houses, and a market for vodun charms.","dontMiss":["The Tamberma tower-houses of Koutammakou","Lomé’s Grand Marché and the Akodessewa fetish market","The hills, waterfalls and coffee farms around Kpalimé"],"lookFor":"Butterflies in the forested hills around Kpalimé, known for their many species.","eat":"Fufu, pounded yam, with groundnut or palm-nut soup.","fact":"Togo has only about 56 km of coastline, but stretches nearly 600 km inland."},
  "TH": {"name":"Thailand","capital":"Bangkok","continent":"Asia","missing":"Street food at every hour, a temple in every town, and limestone islands in warm water.","dontMiss":["The Grand Palace and Wat Pho in Bangkok","Chiang Mai’s old city and the temple on Doi Suthep","The limestone coast of Krabi, or the Similan Islands"],"lookFor":"Wild elephants on the roads and grasslands of Khao Yai National Park, often around dusk.","eat":"Som tam, pounded green papaya salad, with sticky rice and grilled chicken from an Isan stall.","fact":"Bangkok’s full ceremonial name runs to 168 letters, one of the longest place names in the world."},
  "TJ": {"name":"Tajikistan","capital":"Dushanbe","continent":"Asia","missing":"The Pamir Highway over plateaus above 4,000 m, turquoise lakes in the Fann Mountains, and homestays with Pamiri families.","dontMiss":["Driving the Pamir Highway","The Wakhan Valley, looking across the river to Afghanistan","Iskanderkul and the Seven Lakes of the Fann Mountains"],"lookFor":"Yaks on the high pastures of the eastern Pamir around Murghab, often right beside the road.","eat":"Qurutob, flatbread soaked in a dried-yoghurt sauce with onions and herbs, eaten by hand from a shared dish.","fact":"The Fedchenko Glacier, about 77 km long, is the longest glacier outside the polar regions."},
  "TL": {"name":"Timor-Leste","capital":"Dili","continent":"Asia","missing":"Some of the richest reefs on Earth a boat ride from Dili, mountain coffee villages, and almost no other visitors.","dontMiss":["The reefs around Atauro Island","Mount Ramelau at dawn, the country’s highest peak","The Cristo Rei statue and the beaches east of Dili"],"lookFor":"Blue whales and dolphins migrating through the strait off Dili, mostly in October and November.","eat":"Batar daan, a stew of corn, mung beans and pumpkin, and strong local mountain coffee.","fact":"Timor-Leste, independent in 2002, was the first new sovereign state of the 21st century."},
  "TM": {"name":"Turkmenistan","capital":"Ashgabat","continent":"Asia","missing":"A gas crater that has burned in the desert for decades, a capital of white marble, and the ruins of Merv on the Silk Road.","dontMiss":["The Darvaza gas crater at night","The ruined cities of ancient Merv","The Parthian fortress ruins at Nisa"],"lookFor":"Akhal-Teke horses, with their metallic-sheened coats, at the stud farms around Ashgabat.","eat":"Plov, rice with mutton and carrots; and dograma, bread and meat torn into broth.","fact":"Ashgabat holds the Guinness record for the most white marble-clad buildings of any city."},
  "TN": {"name":"Tunisia","capital":"Tunis","continent":"Africa","missing":"Carthage’s ruins by the sea, a Roman amphitheatre almost as big as Rome’s, and Saharan oases where Star Wars was filmed.","dontMiss":["The Roman amphitheatre at El Jem","Sidi Bou Said and the ruins of Carthage","The troglodyte houses of Matmata"],"lookFor":"Date palms by the hundred thousand in the oasis of Tozeur, with the harvest in late autumn.","eat":"Couscous with fish or lamb, brik, thin pastry around a runny egg, and harissa on almost everything.","fact":"Tatooine, Luke Skywalker’s home planet, was named after the southern Tunisian town of Tataouine."},
  "TO": {"name":"Tonga","capital":"Nukuʻalofa","continent":"Oceania","missing":"The Pacific’s only kingdom never formally colonised, humpback whales you can swim beside, and Sundays when the whole country stops.","dontMiss":["Swimming with humpbacks in Vavaʻu, July to October","The Haʻamonga ʻa Maui stone trilithon","The Mapuʻa ʻa Vaca blowholes on Tongatapu"],"lookFor":"Flying foxes hanging in the trees at Kolovai, where they’ve long been protected.","eat":"Ota ika, raw fish in lime and coconut cream; and lu pulu, corned beef baked in taro leaves.","fact":"Tonga keeps its clocks 13 hours ahead of GMT, among the first places on Earth to see each new day."},
  "TR": {"name":"Turkey","capital":"Ankara","continent":"Asia","missing":"Istanbul across two continents, Cappadocia’s cave churches, and a coast full of Greek and Roman ruins.","dontMiss":["Hagia Sophia, then the Bosphorus at sunset","A balloon over Cappadocia at dawn","The Library of Celsus at Ephesus"],"lookFor":"Loggerhead turtles nesting on İztuzu Beach near Dalyan, May to September.","eat":"A long breakfast of cheese, olives, eggs and simit; and kebab, Adana or İskender.","fact":"Göbekli Tepe’s carved stone circles are about 11,500 years old, roughly 6,000 years older than Stonehenge."},
  "TT": {"name":"Trinidad and Tobago","capital":"Port of Spain","continent":"North America","missing":"Carnival at full strength, the steelpan in the place it was invented, and forests full of hummingbirds.","dontMiss":["Carnival in Port of Spain","The verandah at the Asa Wright Nature Centre, for birds","Buccoo Reef and Pigeon Point on Tobago"],"lookFor":"Scarlet ibis flying in to roost at Caroni Swamp at sunset.","eat":"Doubles, curried chickpeas between two pieces of fried flatbread, for breakfast from a roadside stand.","fact":"Pitch Lake in La Brea is the largest natural deposit of asphalt in the world."},
  "TV": {"name":"Tuvalu","capital":"Funafuti","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Nine coral islands with 26 square kilometres of land between them, where the airstrip doubles as the town’s playing field.","dontMiss":["The islets of the Funafuti Conservation Area","Evening football and volleyball on the Funafuti airstrip","A cargo-boat trip to the outer islands"],"lookFor":"Green turtles and reef sharks in the lagoon of the Funafuti Conservation Area.","eat":"Pulaka, a giant swamp taro grown in dug-out pits, cooked with coconut cream.","fact":"Tuvalu earns a large share of its income from licensing its internet domain, .tv."},
  "TW": {"name":"Taiwan","capital":"Taipei","continent":"Asia","missing":"Night markets until late, mountain tea farms above the clouds, and hot springs and temples a metro ride from Taipei.","dontMiss":["A Taipei night market such as Raohe or Shilin","The hillside lanes and teahouses of Jiufen","Sunrise and the forest railway at Alishan"],"lookFor":"Formosan blue magpies, long-tailed and bright blue, in Yangmingshan National Park just north of Taipei.","eat":"Beef noodle soup and xiaolongbao, soup dumplings; stinky tofu at the night markets if you dare.","fact":"Taipei 101 has a 660-tonne steel ball hung near the top that sways to steady the tower in typhoons."},
  "TZ": {"name":"Tanzania","capital":"Dodoma","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Serengeti’s great migration, snow on Kilimanjaro near the equator, and the old spice-trade town on Zanzibar.","dontMiss":["The Serengeti and the Ngorongoro Crater","Climbing Kilimanjaro, or at least seeing it at dawn","The lanes of Stone Town on Zanzibar"],"lookFor":"Wildebeest calving in the southern Serengeti around February, with lions and hyenas close behind.","eat":"Nyama choma, grilled meat, with ugali; and Zanzibar “pizza” from the Forodhani night market.","fact":"Kilimanjaro is the tallest free-standing mountain in the world, rising about 4,900 m above the plains."},
  "UA": {"name":"Ukraine","capital":"Kyiv","continent":"Europe","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: Kyiv’s golden domes, Lviv’s coffee houses, and wooden churches in the Carpathians.","dontMiss":["St Sophia’s Cathedral and the Pechersk Lavra in Kyiv","The old town of Lviv","Kamianets-Podilskyi’s castle on its river loop"],"lookFor":"Sunflower fields across the centre and south of the country in July.","eat":"Borscht with sour cream and pampushky, garlic buns; and varenyky, filled dumplings.","fact":"Kyiv’s Arsenalna station, about 105 m below ground, is one of the deepest metro stations in the world."},
  "UG": {"name":"Uganda","capital":"Kampala","continent":"Africa","missing":"Mountain gorillas, chimps, tree-climbing lions and the source of the Nile, in a country far greener than people expect.","dontMiss":["Gorilla trekking in Bwindi Impenetrable Forest","Murchison Falls, by boat from below and on foot from the top","White-water rafting on the Nile at Jinja"],"lookFor":"Tree-climbing lions resting in the fig trees of Ishasha, in Queen Elizabeth National Park.","eat":"A Rolex: an omelette rolled up in a chapati, from a street stall.","fact":"Uganda is home to roughly half of the world’s mountain gorillas."},
  "US": {"name":"United States","capital":"Washington, D.C.","continent":"North America","missing":"National parks bigger than some countries, road trips across deserts and mountains, and cities with very different food and music.","dontMiss":["The Grand Canyon from the rim at sunset","New York City, on foot","Yellowstone’s geysers and wildlife"],"lookFor":"Bison grazing in Yellowstone’s Lamar and Hayden valleys.","eat":"Barbecue in its regional styles: brisket in Texas, pulled pork in the Carolinas, ribs in Memphis.","fact":"Alaska is the westernmost, northernmost and easternmost US state: its Aleutian Islands cross the 180th meridian."},
  "UY": {"name":"Uruguay","capital":"Montevideo","continent":"South America","missing":"Long, quiet Atlantic beaches, a capital with a riverside promenade for miles, and beef cooked slowly over wood embers.","dontMiss":["Montevideo’s Rambla and Ciudad Vieja","The cobbled old town of Colonia del Sacramento","Cabo Polonio, a village with no roads and no mains power"],"lookFor":"Southern right whales close to shore off Punta del Este and Rocha, from July to November.","eat":"Asado from the parrilla, and the chivito, a steak sandwich piled with ham, egg and cheese.","fact":"Uruguay hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup, in 1930."},
  "UZ": {"name":"Uzbekistan","capital":"Tashkent","continent":"Asia","missing":"Blue-tiled Silk Road cities, madrasas you can walk into, and plov cooked in giant cauldrons to feed whole neighbourhoods.","dontMiss":["Samarkand’s Registan at dusk","The old centre of Bukhara around Lyab-i Hauz","Itchan Kala, the walled inner town of Khiva"],"lookFor":"Mulberry trees, grown for silkworms, around the silk workshops of Margilan in the Fergana Valley.","eat":"Plov, rice with lamb, yellow carrots and cumin cooked in a kazan, best eaten at lunch.","fact":"Uzbekistan is one of only two doubly landlocked countries; the other is Liechtenstein."},
  "VA": {"name":"Vatican City","capital":"Vatican City","continent":"Europe","missing":"Michelangelo’s Sistine Chapel ceiling, St Peter’s dome, and the papal art collections, all in a country under half a square kilometre.","dontMiss":["The Sistine Chapel, first thing in the morning","Climbing to the top of St Peter’s dome","The Vatican Gardens, on a guided tour"],"lookFor":"Yellow-legged gulls on the statues of St Peter’s colonnade; one famously sat on the conclave chimney in 2013.","eat":"There’s no Vatican cuisine, but supplì and pizza al taglio are a short walk away in Rome’s Prati.","fact":"The Swiss Guard has protected the pope since 1506."},
  "VC": {"name":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","capital":"Kingstown","continent":"North America","missing":"A green volcanic island and a chain of little Grenadines, where sailing means short hops between empty beaches and reefs.","dontMiss":["The Tobago Cays and their turtles","Admiralty Bay on Bequia","Hiking La Soufrière volcano"],"lookFor":"Green turtles grazing the seagrass off Baradal in the Tobago Cays.","eat":"Roasted breadfruit and fried jackfish, the national dish.","fact":"Breadfruit came to St Vincent with Captain Bligh in 1793, after the Bounty mutiny ruined his first try."},
  "VE": {"name":"Venezuela","capital":"Caracas","continent":"South America","missing":"When it’s safer to go again: the world’s highest waterfall, flat-topped tepui mountains, and a Caribbean coast with coral islands.","dontMiss":["Angel Falls, by river from Canaima","Climbing Roraima, the tepui on the Brazilian border","The Los Roques archipelago"],"lookFor":"Capybaras by the hundred and scarlet ibis in the Llanos wetlands, in the dry season.","eat":"Arepas, split corn cakes stuffed with cheese, black beans or shredded beef.","fact":"Lake Maracaibo gets more lightning than anywhere else on Earth, with storms on up to 160 nights a year."},
  "VI": {"name":"U.S. Virgin Islands","capital":"Charlotte Amalie","continent":"North America","missing":"St John’s national park beaches, Danish colonial towns, and coral reefs you can snorkel straight from shore.","dontMiss":["The underwater snorkel trail at Trunk Bay on St John","Christiansted’s Danish fort on St Croix","Buck Island Reef, by boat from St Croix"],"lookFor":"Green turtles grazing the seagrass at Maho Bay on St John.","eat":"Pates, fried pastries filled with saltfish or spiced beef; and fungi, cornmeal, with fish.","fact":"The US bought the islands from Denmark in 1917 for $25 million in gold, and cars still drive on the left."},
  "VN": {"name":"Vietnam","capital":"Hanoi","continent":"Asia","missing":"Noodle soup at plastic tables on the pavement, limestone bays in the north, and a coast you can follow by train for 1,700 km.","dontMiss":["A night on a boat in Ha Long Bay or Lan Ha Bay","Hội An’s old town by lantern light","The caves of Phong Nha"],"lookFor":"Red-shanked douc langurs in the forest of the Sơn Trà peninsula, just outside Da Nang.","eat":"Phở for breakfast, and bánh mì, the baguette sandwich, from a street cart.","fact":"Sơn Đoòng, in Phong Nha, is the largest known cave passage on Earth, with its own jungle inside."},
  "VU": {"name":"Vanuatu","capital":"Port Vila","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Over 80 islands with more than 100 languages, kava at dusk, and an active volcano you can walk up to the rim of.","dontMiss":["Mount Yasur’s crater on Tanna at dusk","Land diving on Pentecost, April to June","The blue holes and Champagne Beach on Espiritu Santo"],"lookFor":"Dugongs grazing the seagrass in Lamen Bay, on Epi Island.","eat":"Lap lap, grated yam, taro or banana baked in banana leaves with coconut cream.","fact":"Vanuatu has an underwater post office off Hideaway Island; you post waterproof postcards with a snorkel on."},
  "WS": {"name":"Samoa","capital":"Apia","continent":"Oceania","missing":"Villages of open-sided fale, waterfalls and swimming holes inland, and a pace of life set by church and family.","dontMiss":["To Sua Ocean Trench, a swimming hole down a ladder","A night in a beach fale at Lalomanu","Robert Louis Stevenson’s house at Vailima"],"lookFor":"Samoan flying foxes, which feed by day, over the forests of Savai’i.","eat":"Palusami, taro leaves wrapped around coconut cream and baked in an umu, a stone oven.","fact":"Samoa skipped 30 December 2011 entirely, jumping across the date line to line up with Australia and New Zealand."},
  "XK": {"name":"Kosovo","capital":"Pristina","continent":"Europe","missing":"Serbian Orthodox monasteries, Ottoman Prizren, mountain walks near Peja, and the youngest population in Europe.","dontMiss":["Prizren’s old town and the fortress above it","Visoki Dečani Monastery","The Rugova Gorge near Peja"],"lookFor":"Brown bears at the Bear Sanctuary Prishtina, rescued from captivity and living in woodland enclosures.","eat":"Flija, dozens of thin batter layers baked slowly under a lid of hot coals, served with yoghurt.","fact":"Pristina has a 3 m statue of Bill Clinton, on Bill Clinton Boulevard."},
  "YE": {"name":"Yemen","capital":"Sanaa","continent":"Asia","missing":"When it’s safe to go again: mud-brick tower houses in Sanaa and Shibam, and the dragon’s blood trees of Socotra.","dontMiss":["The old city of Sanaa","The mud-brick high-rises of Shibam","The Dixam plateau on Socotra"],"lookFor":"Dragon’s blood trees, shaped like upturned umbrellas, found only on Socotra.","eat":"Saltah, a bubbling stew of meat and frothy fenugreek, eaten with flatbread at lunch.","fact":"Shibam’s 16th-century mud-brick towers, some about 30 m tall, earned it the name “Manhattan of the desert”."},
  "ZA": {"name":"South Africa","capital":"Pretoria","continent":"Africa","missing":"The Big Five, a coast with whales and penguins, wine valleys under mountains, and Cape Town on its peninsula.","dontMiss":["Table Mountain, by cable car or up Platteklip Gorge","A safari in Kruger or a reserve on its border","Robben Island, where Mandela was held"],"lookFor":"African penguins at Boulders Beach near Simon’s Town, all year.","eat":"A braai of boerewors and chops, and in Durban, bunny chow: curry in a hollowed-out loaf.","fact":"South Africa has three capitals: Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein."},
  "ZM": {"name":"Zambia","capital":"Lusaka","continent":"Africa","missing":"Victoria Falls from the side where you can swim at the edge, walking safaris in the Luangwa Valley, and canoeing past hippos.","dontMiss":["Devil’s Pool, on the lip of Victoria Falls, in the dry season","A walking safari in South Luangwa","Canoeing the Lower Zambezi"],"lookFor":"Millions of straw-coloured fruit bats arriving at Kasanka National Park, November to December.","eat":"Nshima, a stiff maize porridge, with relishes of greens, beans or small dried kapenta fish.","fact":"Lake Kariba, shared with Zimbabwe, is the largest man-made reservoir in the world by volume."},
  "ZW": {"name":"Zimbabwe","capital":"Harare","continent":"Africa","missing":"The widest view of Victoria Falls, the stone ruins that gave the country its name, and Mana Pools, where guides walk you near elephants.","dontMiss":["Victoria Falls from the rainforest path","The stone ruins of Great Zimbabwe","Mana Pools on the Zambezi"],"lookFor":"Elephants at Hwange’s waterholes in the dry season; the park holds over 40,000 of them.","eat":"Sadza, a thick maize porridge, with stewed beef and greens.","fact":"Zimbabwe has 16 official languages, among the most of any country."},
};
